'''Balisca''' ({{Audio|bl-Balisca.ogg|pronunciation}}; {{lang-ku|Anhangá}}; {{Lang-arn|Quetrupillán|lit=sleeping {{W|Pillan|pillán}}}}), officially the '''Baliscan Confederation''' ({{Langx|bl|Confederación Baliscana}}) is a country located within the [[Northern Triangle]] of the continent of [[Abyala]]. Balisca is the most populous country in the [[Western Hemisphere]], and the largest by total area, spanning a total {{Convert|4688451.45|km2|sqmi|abbr=on}}. The capital and most-populous city of Balisca is [[Castejón]]. The country's [[W:Working language|working language]] is [[Baliscano]], which is the world's most widely spoken [[W:Creole language|creole language]]. Balisca is one of the most multicultural and ethnically diverse nations, a result of substantial immigration from various places across the world. It shares land borders with [[Asikyira]] and [[Potocsí]]. Balisca has maintained a historic claim to a [[Baliscan Arktici|significant portion of Arktici]], though it has suspended this claim inline with it's obligations under the [[Arktici Treaty System]].

Originally, what is now Balisca was settled by various indigenous tribes before being explored by a number of Illypnian expeditions during the 16th and 17th centuries, most notably by the [[Bizkarra|Bizkarran]] explorer [[Xenokrates Lizoain]] under charter from the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania]]. Following the consolidation of colonial sovereignty over the [[Northern Triangle]] through a [[Treaties of Ampuria|series of concession agreements]], the territory was explored through leased territories granted to [[Xacan|Xacano interests]], [[Kastruneto|Kastrunetian trading companies]], and various Mesogean merchant republics operating under Volisanian license. Relative to other Volisanian colonial possessions, particularly the wealthy trading posts in the eastern Mesogean, the footholds within the Northern Triangle were considered exceptionally "poor and dangerous" places during the 16th and 17th centuries.    

The [[Bayani Revolt]] (1568-1571) in the Xacano homelands and the subsequent expulsion of Bayani converts prompted large-scale resettlement to the Xacano concessions in Balisca, fundamentally altering the demographic and linguistic character of the colony. The [[devolution of the United Kingdoms of Volisania]] following the [[Volisanian Civil War]] (1736-1801) and the establishment of the [[Community of Volisania]] under the [[Law of the Community of Volisania Acts 1801 and 1812]] created a period of colonial autonomy known as the "[[Volisanya Boba]]" ("meek Volisania"), during which the Baliscan concessions operated with minimal metropolitan oversight. 

The emergence of a centralizing faction within the newly reconstituted Community of Volisania, hostile to the semi-autonomous status of the Baliscan concessions, eviscerated what little remaining political sway colonial loyalists and metropolitan-born administrators held. Volisania would later launch an [[Baliscan War of Independence|attempt at re-asserting imperial authority]] in 1808, seeking to integrate the concessions directly into the Commonwealth structure. In response to the Volisanian aggression, 18 (later rising to 23) colonial communities and the [[Hesperian League]] issued the [[Proclamation of Alcazaba]], uniting as the [[Northern Confederation]] to wage their resistance against Volisanian reconquest. This confederation left all major functions to the individual [[Communities of Balisca|communities]], establishing the decentralized political structure that would characterize the future Baliscan Confederation. 

[[Confederal Question|Discontent]] with this system of government arose immediately after the end of hostilities, especially in regards to tariffs, trade, and taxes. These tensions erupted into the [[Pastry War]], between Volisanian and [[Bayara|Xacano]] colonists and prompted [[Confederal emergency|confederal intervention]], ultimately sparking an [[Baliscan Civil War|extended civil war]] that lasted until 1838. The development of a [[Cosmocazo|cosmopolitan]] identity during the [[Anyos Federales|Reconstruction era]] resulted in significant strides in Baliscan socioeconomic development. It underwent a [[W:Futurism|futurist]] [[Baliscan Revolution|social revolution]] in the aftermath of the [[W:First World War|First World War]], from which it emerged as the world's largest [[W:Libertarian socialist|libertarian socialist]] society. It would later experiment with [[Accelerationism|accelerationist]] and [[W:Futurism|futurist]] theory that resulted in substantial [[Baliscan values|social and cultural shifts]].

Balisca ranks among the highest in international measurements of {{W|government transparency}}, {{W|civil liberties}}, {{W|quality of life}}, {{W|social development}}, and {{W|education}}. As of 2022, Balisca is described as being highly democratic, with an {{W|open-source governance|open-source}}-system of governance that promotes universal {{W|participatory democracy|participation}}, beginning at the lowest level in it's {{W|administrative division}}s. This includes widespread support for a universal [[Social protection in Balisca|social protection system]] aimed specifically at enhancing individual autonomy and promoting {{W|social mobility}}. This {{W|cooperative economics|cooperative system}} involves the integration of [[Communes of Balisca|communes]] in administration, and a commitment to {{W|social ownership}} based on {{W|Mutualism (economic theory)|mutualism}}, with a large number of {{W|state-owned enterprises}} and cooperative ownership in [[Essential industry|essential industries]]. As of 2020, Balisca ranks highly on the inequality-adjusted HDI, as well as being ranked in the top 10 on both the {{W|Social Progress Index}} and the {{W|World Happiness Report}}.

Balisca is a {{W|Developed country|highly developed country}}, and it's [[Economy of Balisca|economy]] is the world's fourth-[[wikipedia:List of countries by GDP (nominal)|largest by nominal GDP]] and fourth-[[wikipedia:List of countries by GDP (PPP)|largest by GDP (PPP)]]. Since the late 1950s, it has operated a [[W:Decentralized planning|decentralized planned]] economy which synthesizes {{W|Mutualism (economic theory)|mutualist ideals}} with a [[W:Market socialist|market socialist]] orientation. It's labor force is among the world's largest, most educated, and the [[Trade unions in Balisca|most unionized]]. The Baliscan government has followed a policy of {{W|military neutrality|international neutrality}} through {{W|non-alignment}} since 1938, although this was violated by [[Potocsí]] during the [[Camboriú War]]. Balisca is a founding member of the [[Adonian Community]], the [[Abyalan Mutual Development Community]], the [[Pan-Andalusian Community]], and the [[COCO|Concepción Organization of Cooperation]]. Balisca is recognized [[w:Great power|great power]] in international affairs, and is widely regarded as an [[w:Potential superpower|emerging]] "{{W|soft power|soft superpower}}". 

==Etymology==
[[File:Açaí berries.webp|left|thumb|266x266px|[[W:Açaí palm#Fruit|Açaí berries]], the fruit of [[W:Euterpe oleracea|''Euterpe oleracea'']], from which the country takes it's name]]
The first Illypnians to step foot in what is now Balisca arrived on 2 June 1500, with the arrival of a fleet flying the flag of Andalus and commanded by the Bizkarran explorer [[Xenokrates Lizoain]] at [[Baía de Vera Creu]]. Lizoain's expedition met various indigenous tribes, many who spoke related languages of the [[Kurupiran-Nyengatú languages|Kurupiran-Nyengatú family]]. 

Lizoain, upon further exploration and discoveries eventually named the entire land  ''Patagoi basilika'' (or "Balisca of the [[W:Patagons|Patagons]] [big feet]"), in reference to the natives he encountered that engaged in the harvesting of [[W:Açaí#Fruit|açaí berries]] (which the Illypnians said resembled the appearance of [[W:La Rioja wine|ancient grapes]] from Lizoain's homeland, [[Bizkarra]]). Lizoain established a close friendship with native leaders, especially that of the [[Kurupiranga people|Kurupiranga]] peoples originating from [[Pindorama]] and the skilled [[W:Caiçara|Caiçara]] sailors, whom he viewed as very important partners in the exploration of Balisca. Lizoain would make three trips between the Mesogean and the Baliscan territories.  

In the face of the [[Volisanian colonial empire]]'s intention to reassert colonial rule in Balisca, the various republics came together in the [[Articles of Confederation]] to form the [[Confederation of the Northern Provinces]]. 

The [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|Indigenous]] [[W:Mapuche language|Walldungu]] name ''Quetrupillán'' ({{Literal translation|sleeping {{W|pillán}}}}) and [[Kurupiran language|Kurupirangüe]] ''Anhangá'' (''Ahiaḡ'' in the Kurupirangüe script; {{Literal translation|land of spirits}}) are the pre-colonial names of Balisca, both referencing the traditional indigenous view of the [[Northern Triangle]] as the home of spirits of the land that also served as the protector of the fauna and flora, who "[...] doesn't devour nor kill, but avenges animals victimized by insatiable hunters". 

==History==
{{Main|History of Balisca}}

=== Indigenous peoples and Illypnian contact ===
{{Main|Indigenous peoples in Balisca|Matarrañyan civilizations}}

{{multiple image|direction=horizontal|align=right|width1=148|width2=150|footer=|image1=India tupi.jpg|caption1=A [[Guanarú]] (western [[Kurupiranga]]) woman with a child|image2=Kurupiranga man.jpg|caption2=A [[Ypérynã]] (northeastern Kurupiranga) man}}
At the time of Mesogean arrival, the territory of modern-day western Balisca had as many as 2,000 tribes. The Indigenous peoples were traditionally mostly semi-nomadic tribes that subsisted on hunting, fishing, gathering, and migrant agriculture. When the Volisanians arrived in 1500, the Natives lived mainly on the coast and along the banks of major rivers, with "the earliest going back at least 10,000 years in the highlands of [[Pindorama]]". The dating of the origins of the first inhabitants, who were called "[[W:Patagons|Patagons]]" (''patagón'') by the Mesogeans, is still a matter of dispute among archaeologists. The earliest pottery ever found in Balisca, radiocarbon-dated 8,000 years old, has been excavated in the territory of the former [[Tapuya Confederacy]], near [[Yvyra'ara]], providing evidence to overturn the assumption that the tropical forest region was too poor in resources to have supported a complex prehistoric culture.

The current most widely accepted view of anthropologists, linguists and geneticists is that the early tribes were part of the first wave of [[W:Nomad|nomadic]] [[W:Hunter-gatherer|hunter-gatherers]] who came into the Algarves from [[Fosia]], either by land, across the [[W:Bering Strait|x Strait]], or by sea routes across the [[Shendan Ocean]], or both.

The formative period of [[Basabyala]] is one of the [[TBA#]] independent [[W:Cradles of civilization|cradles of civilization]], this era saw the origin of distinct cultural traits such as religious and symbolic traditions, [[W:Potato|potato]] and [[W:Quinoa|quinoa]] cultivation, artistic and architectural complexes that spread from [[Matarrañyan civilizations|Matarrañyan cultures]] to the rest of the [[Marañón basin]]. Villages became more densely populated, socially stratified with an artisan class, and developed into chiefdoms. The most powerful rulers had religious and political power, organizing the construction of ceremonial centers.

The [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] on the east coast of Abyala created a rather sharp cultural boundary between the settled agrarian civilizations of the east coast and the semi-nomadic tribes of the west, who never developed written records or permanent monumental architecture. Archaeological remains (mainly pottery) indicate a complex pattern of regional cultural developments, internal migrations, and occasional large [[W:Confederation#Indigenous confederations in South America|state-like confederations]]. This included the aforementioned [[Tapuya]], and the [[Tapajóara culture]] (River Alliance), which came to control much of the Lower [[Marañón River]] [[Marañón basin|basin]], through a combination of agricultural innovation, riverine trade networks, and military conquest.
[[File:80_-_Machu_Picchu_-_Juin_2009_-_crop.jpg|left|thumb|[[Itá-Marangatu]] ({{Literal translation|sacred stone}}) was a religious site and [[W:Pilgrimage|pilgrimage]] destination of the [[Aruanãyára Empire#Religion|Aruanãyára]]]]
In the 15th century, the [[Aruanãyára Empire|Aruanãyára emerged as a powerful state]] which, in the span of a century, formed one of the largest empire in the [[pre-colonial Abyala]] with their capital in [[Takuakesetyba|Takûakesétyba]]. This group represented one of the subgroups of the wider [[Kurupiranga|Kurupiranga ethnic groups]], which had dispersed throughout the [[Marañón basin]]. Gradually, as early as the thirteenth century, they began to expand and incorporate their neighbors. Aruanãyáran expansion was slow until about the middle of the fifteenth century, when the pace of conquest began to accelerate, particularly under the rule of the emperor [[TBA]]. The Aruanãyárans came to control most of the [[Matarrañya|Southern Matarrañyan]] region, ruling over a population of 5 to 10 million inhabitants.

From 1460 to 1520, the Aruanãyára Empire used a variety of methods, from violent conquest to peaceful assimilation, to incorporate a large portion of eastern Abyala, centered on the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]], from northern [[Lacashe]] to the southern Alpujarra, between the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] in the west and the [[Marañón rainforest]]  in the east. The official language of the empire was [[Classical Aruanãyára]], although hundreds of local languages and dialects were spoken. They referred to their empire as ''Itá-Rapé-Retã'' which can be translated as "Land of the Stone Path." Many local forms of worship persisted in the empire, most of them concerning local sacred spirits, but the Aruanãyára leadership encouraged the worship of [[W:Tupã|''Tupã'']], manifestation of god through thunder, and imposed its sovereignty above other cults such as that of [[W:Jurupari|Jurupari]]. The Aruanãyára [[W:Imperial cult|considered their leader]] to be ''Rupave'' "father of the people" to be a [[W:Demigod|demigod]].

Troops of the Aruanãyára Empire are reported to have reached [[Alagón River|Alagón river]] and fought with the local [[Kuarupíba]]. Thus, the northern border of the Empire is believed by most modern scholars to be situated somewhere between [[Castejón]] and the [[Akûtukû Desert]].

The [[W:Mapuche|Wallche]] inhabited the northern regions of Tinguiririca, particularly around present-day [[Cadaqués]] and [[Dríadas]]. Their society was agricultural, cultivating [[W:Maize|maize]], [[W:Beans|beans]], [[W:Squash|squash]], and [[W:Potatoes|potatoes]], and to a lesser extent practiced hunting and fishing. They developed advanced farming techniques, including terracing and irrigation, allowing them to sustain large populations. The Wallche also engaged in sophisticated forms of social organization, with each village, or ''lof'', led by a chief, or ''lonko''. They practiced a [[W:Mapuche religion|polytheistic religion]] with a rich mythology, deeply connected to the natural world. In contrast, the [[W:Yaghan people|Yamana people]] lived a nomadic lifestyle as hunter-gatherers in the southern [[W:Fjord|fjords]] and [[W:Ria|rias]]. They were skilled mariners, using [[W:Dugout canoe|dugout canoes]] and [[W:Dalca|dalcas]]—boats made from planks sewn together with natural fibers—to navigate the complex waterways. The Yamna relied on fishing, hunting sea mammals, and gathering shellfish and wild plants. Their communities were small and highly mobile, adapting to the harsh, variable climate of southern Cerdanya. The Yamana had a profound spiritual connection to the sea and their environment, with intricate rituals and oral traditions passed down through generations.
[[File:Square_rigged_caravels.jpg|thumb|The [[W:Square-rigged caravel|square-rigged caravels]] of [[Xenokrates Lizoain]]'s armada of 1500 that landed at [[Baía de Vera Creu]]]]
The first Illypnian to step foot in what is now Balisca arrived on 2 June 1500, with the arrival of an [[United Kingdoms of Volisania|Volisanian]]-chartered fleet commanded by the [[Bizkarra|Bizkarran]] explorer [[Xenokrates Lizoain]] at [[Baía de Vera Creu]] (within present-day [[Piedmont]]). The explorers first encountered the Guanarú tribe, which spoke a dialect of the [[W:Tupi–Guarani languages|Kurupiran-Nyengatú]] languages. The initial encounter between the settlers and the indigenous tribes was a delicate balance of curiosity and caution, with the natives welcoming Lizoain and his crew with a [[W:Tearful salutation|tearful salutation]]. Upon sailing around the [[Tinguirirican Peninsula]] and discovering the [[Marañón Delta]], Lizoain named the entire land ''Patagoi basilika'' (or "Balisca of the [[W:Patagons|Patagons]]"), in reference to the big feet (''pata'') of the natives (presumed to be [[W:Yaghan people|Yamana]]) he encountered in the north, and the [[W:Acai berries|acai berries]] which had a similar appearance to that of the [[W:History of La Rioja wine|bygone ancient grape vines]] from his homeland. Lizoain maintained a cordial relationship with native leaders, especially that of the [[Tapajóara culture|Tapajóara peoples]] originating from [[Pindorama]] and the skilled [[W:Caiçara|Caiçara]] sailors, whom he viewed as instrumental in the exploration of the [[Marañón basin|Marañón]].

