[[File:Balisca direct action.jpg|thumb|250px|{{W|Direct action}} and {{W|grassroots activism}} are prominent features of the Baliscan political scene.]]Balisca's political system is founded on [[W:Libertarian socialism|libertarian socialist]] theory, and facilitating {{W|decentralization}} and {{W|localism|local autonomy}} are core tenets of confederal politics. In it's present form (since 2022), the confederation is an extremely {{W|decentralization|decentralized}}, {{W|Participatory democracy|universally participatory}}, {{W|collaborative e-democracy}}, often labelled a {{W|liquid democracy}} (incorporating aspects of [[W:Direct democracy|direct democracy]] and [[W:Representative democracy|representative democracy]]). 

The [[Secretary General of Balisca|Secretary General]] (SecGen) is the chair of the [[Secretariat Council (Balisca)|Secretariat Council]], the ''de facto'' executive branch. The SecGen is considered to be "first among equals", and is representative of the confederal-level administration in Balisca internationally. The incumbent is [[Zavier Bilal]], who was first appointed in July 2021. The SecGen recommends individuals to the [[Confederal Congress|Congress]] to be appointed to the Secretariat Council. Deliberative councils in each administrative entity are the main [[W:Anarchist law|source of law]] in Balisca.

The [[Confederal Congress]] is the "highest form of democratic authority", consisting of 736 delegates elected by their [[Commune district|commune districts]]. According to the [[w:Democracy Index|2022 Democracy Index]], Balisca garnered a score of 9.64, ranking it a full democracy.

The fundamental linchpins of this system are the principles of {{W|open-source governance}} and the "[[W:E-democracy|wikidemocracy]]" approach. This facilitates the rapid transmission of relevant information from the source of the discussion to the relevant populace, via the ''[[Cosmo]]'' system and automated information sorting software, which also employs human engineers assigned to assess unusual problems, problem trends, or through an appeal process pursued by an individual. [[W:Radical transparency|Radical transparency]] guides political discourse, often leading to intense public discussion. Arbitration and mediation proceedings, decisions, and agreements are live-streamed and fully open to the public to ensure accountability and allow for community scrutiny. 

Baliscans have called this system the functional {{W|antithesis}} of traditional bureaucracies which sometimes notoriously suffer from {{W|w:corruption|corruption}}, slowness, and a list of other unfortunate qualities. In addition, this open-sourced approach is far more adaptive with its focus on {{W|participatory democracy|participation}}, {{W|direct democracy}} and cultural contexts compared to traditional bureaucracies. The [[Open Cabildo]] utilizes {{W|Government by algorithm|administrative algorithms and AI}}.

As a matter of principle, '''all non-direct democratic authority in Balisca is exercised through the election of authority'''. This means the people elect ("delegate") the executive and legislative authorities, with the stipulation they are all subject to potential recall. Economically, this means the workers within an sector elect their coordinating figures, and the workers within a particular enterprise elect the chairpersons and executive representatives. Elections of delegates by the people or by members of organizations (e.g. syndicate members voting to elect their administrative representatives) are always by {{W|secret ballot}}. On the other hand, all votes cast by the elected delegates (on any level) on various matters, including the election of ministers, are always cast in public, and the votes recorded, even though their deliberations sometimes are closed. 

Balisca today is considered a [[w:Consensus democracy|consensus democracy]], where consensus is an important feature of political culture. Aspects of [[W:Direct democracy|direct democracy]] have been well ingrained in the Baliscan political process since [[Baliscan War of Independence|confederation]], in the form of the [[w:Open cabildo|earliest open cabildos]]. Since 2022, the constitution also specifically encourages the direct participation of citizens through [[W:Popular assembly|virtual assemblies]] on the Open Cabildo. A [[Confederal Convention]] must occur at least once every ten years in order to re-examine the constitution's content and potentially revise or replace it through amendments according to the confederation's shifting needs, though they often take place more frequently. 
[[File:Confederal Council of Arbitration building.png|left|thumb|300x300px|[[Palacio Arbitraje|Palacio Arbitrage]], the seat of the [[Confederal Council of Arbitration]]]]
The judiciary operates based on principles that prioritize individual freedom, social equality, and direct democratic participation, and accordingly, the judiciary is organized at multiple levels, ranging from local communal courts to regional and federated courts, depending on the scale of decision-making required. As such, judicial matters are administered primarily by [[Reconciliation council (Balisca)|reconciliation councils]], although conflicts and disputes that were unable to be resolved at lower levels may make their way up to the [[Confederal Council of Arbitration]], which (through the voluntary consent of the cantons to participate in and be bound by the decisions of the Council) exercises jurisdiction confederation-wide. It is important to note that the specific structure and functioning of the judiciary in Balisca varies based on local contexts and community preferences. These principles serve as general guidelines, but the actual implementation is consistently undergoing experimentation, evolution, and democratic decision-making. 

A strong emphasis is placed on {{W|transformative justice}}, which focuses on addressing the root causes of harm rather than resorting to [[W:Punishment|punitive measures]]. {{W|Restorative justice}} approaches, such as mediation, reconciliation, and community service form the foundation of the Baliscan judicial system. The aim of this system is to repair harm, rehabilitate offenders, and reintegrate them into the community, rather than relying primarily on imprisonment. Additionally, decisions pertaining to guilt or innocence and suitable consequences are made by a jury of peers, rather than a single judge. This approach ensures collective decision-making that reflects the values and perspectives of the respective community. Jurors are selected through a transparent and inclusive process, actively participating in determining case outcomes.

===Law===
{{Main|Basic Laws of Balisca|Constitution of Balisca}}

{{W|Anarchist law}} concepts prevail over both {{W|civil law|civil}} and {{W|common law}} practice. Under the Baliscan radical decentralized scheme, the bulk of "law" is adopted at the communal, cantonal, and regional levels via {{W|citizens' assembly|citizens' assemblies}}. This includes criminal law, civil law, education, water resources law, land allocation, housing and leasing, and highways. But if a confederal level initiatives and a particular law contradict one another, it is by no means certain that the national law will prevail.  One must go to Article 4 of the constitution to see the boundaries between federal and regional authorities.  And these boundaries are drawn so that in many if not most conflicts are resolved in favor of regional power. This is regarded as a radical democratic principle, the devolution of power downward to the levels of {{W|Libertarian municipalism|government closest to the people}}.

Any discourse pertaining to Balisca law necessitates the inclusion of the fundamental principles known as the {{W|Right of revolution|right to revolt}} and the {{W|right to resist}}. These principles protect individuals' right to resist any violation of their {{W|unenumerated rights}}. Consequently, an individual can present a defense in court by asserting that they violated a law due to its inherent injustice, thereby challenging the legitimacy of the law itself. These established the principle that no law is beyond scrutiny. Moreover, this right has led to a particularly active culture of {{W|direct action}}, such as {{W|détournement}}, {{W|riots}}, sit-ins, and {{W|self-managed social centers}} and {{W|squatting|building occupations}}.

[[Anarchism in Balisca]] grew out of Balisca's tradition of {{W|direct democracy}}, beginning with {{W|open cabildos}} and eventually [[W:Soviet democracy|council democracy]]. This led to the idea of ''bien fortificad'' or ''democracia lesta per batalla'' ({{Literal translation|well-fortified|battle-ready democracy}}) which implies that each individual is empowered to defend the [[W:Horizontalidad|horizontal]] and [[W:Consensus democracy|consensus-based order]] (''orden horizontal y consenso basado'') against those who want to abolish it, but only as a last resort. The idea behind the concept is the notion that even a {{W|majority rule}} of the people cannot be allowed to install an authoritative, {{W|Totalitarianism|totalitarian}} or {{W|autocratic}} regime capable of violating the principles of the Baliscan constitution and the [[Basic Laws of Balisca|Basic Laws]].

While the legal system is based on the [[Constitution of Balisca|Baliscan Constitution]], most recently promulgated on 23 July 2022, the basic principles of the [[w:Rule of law|rule of law]] were laid out in the [[Basic Laws of Balisca]], which were voluntarily adopted by cantons in the aftermath of the [[Baliscan Revolution]]. All other initiatives and reconciliation decisions must conform to these rules. As of July 2022, the constitution has been re-written 12 times, and has had 74 amendments. The electorate is the main source of statutes (via {{W|direct democracy}} and the [[Open Cabildo]]), although in certain matters confederal legislative and executive bodies may temporarily enact legislation.

This is reflected in the Congress, which is officially {{W|nonpartisan}}. In Balisca's iteration of {{W|liquid democracy}}, local and regional delegates can be recalled at anytime, and are bound to vote as instructed by their constituents.

This system has been praised over the last few decades for the relatively quick pace of decision-making and its encouragement of {{W|Civic engagement}}. Lawsuits on appeal are typically resolved in lower courts of appeal. In 2006, [[Confederal Council of Arbitration|Council of Arbitration]] was the first court in the world to transmit its sessions on television, and also via {{W|YouTube}}. More recently, in December 2022, the Constitutional Council joined the [[Open Cabildo]] to display items on the day planner of the ministers, and to inform the daily actions of the court and it's docket.
====Elections and voting====
The voting age in Balisca is 16, as established by a referendum approved by 56% of voters in 1976, while maintaining the [[W:Age of majority|age of majority]] (18) across the confederation. Voting is mandatory for all persons aged 18-65, and those that do not vote are penalized with a Ꭶ150 fine, while reoffenders can receive fines up to S/550. This was retained by popular vote, with 65% in favor in a 2012 referendum. As a result, Balisca still has some of the highest voter turnout in the world, averaging above 80% consistently since the 1990s, and reaching a record high of 93.2% of eligible voters participating in the [[2021 Baliscan confederal election|2021 election]].

Once a person settles in Balisca, they are required by law to register with the {{W|e-government}} portal of the commune they reside in within two weeks, so the local councils will know their voting eligibility, thus eliminating the need for them to register to vote themselves. In the weeks before a referendum, or an election, electronic ballot cards are sent out to every eligible voter, who casts their vote, verifies their identity to prevent fraud, and sends it back to the municipality. The transmission is checked to verify the voter, and is disposed of (deleted), and the ballot card is collected, thus making the process autonomous and remote. As of 2021, all Baliscan commune districts have introduced [[w:Electronic voting|electronic voting]], allowing people to cast their votes or propose popular initiatives over the internet or through SMS.

Balisca extends voting rights to citizens of fellow [[COCO]] member states who have lived in the country for a minimum of two years, while all other foreigners have been allowed to vote in [[W:Local elections|local elections]] since 2002. Besides these two exceptions, all other forms of voting are restricted to Baliscan citizens.

===Administrative divisions===
{{See also|Communes of Balisca|Cantons of Balisca|Regions of Balisca|Communities of Balisca}}Balisca is a {{W|confederation}} consisting of forty-four [[Regions of Balisca|regions]] (based on [[W:Bioregionalism|bioregionalism]]), which consist of 3,904 [[cantons of Balisca|cantons]], and are further divided into 57,681 highly autonomous [[Communes of Balisca|communes]]. Cantons within the [[Castejón Metropolis]] are referred to as "boroughs" and they are divided into ''barrios''. 

Communes are arguably the most powerful level of political organization in Balisca, due to historical {{W|decentralization}} and adherence to concepts such as {{W|libertarian municipalism}} and [[W:Municipal socialism|municipal socialism]], based on highly {{W|participatory democracy|participatory}} {{W|grassroots politics}} in which local communities democratically plan and manage their affairs through {{W|popular assembly|popular assemblies}}. This democratic deliberation purposefully promotes autonomy and self-reliance, as opposed to centralized state politics. While this program retains elements of anarchism, it emphasizes a higher degree of organization (community planning, voting, and institutions) than general anarchism. In this form of communalism, these autonomous, communities connect with each other to form cantons. The number of cantons is not static, meaning cantons can be dissolved and new one's can be created at any pursuant to approval via popular assemblies {{W|popular assembly|citizens'/popular assemblies}}. 

Baliscans primarily wield their self-determination through communes and cantons (explaining the lack of any political role of the [[Communities of Balisca|communities]] [which have cultural/ceremonial competencies only]). For any government to revoke the autonomy of these councils is a near-impossibility, though nothing bars it legally. Additionally, the regions have full control over cooperative spending, transferring payments to the confederal administration for the common services (military, international relations, macroeconomic policy), they developed their own {{W|legal culture}}, freely organize their local governments, and own and manage their natural and financial resources. All of the constituent territories exercise {{W|liquid democracy}} through both council delegates and {{W|direct democracy|direct}} {{W|participatory democracy|participation}} of the people.
