Balisca consists of seven historic [[W:Ethnolinguistics|cultural linguistic]] communities within the borders of Balisca. They emerged in the midst of [[Volisanian Civil War|the implosion]] of the [[Volisanian Empire]] in the 1810s, while the original three communities ([[Bayara]], [[Hesperia]], and [[Cerdanya]]) were [[W:Thalassocracy|thalassocratic]] network of settlements that voluntarily {{W|confederation|confederated}} with mutual recognition of the right to {{W|self-determination}}. Powers varied between these communities varied and largely depended on their economic power and {{W|geography|geographic}} location. Through the adoption of confederal compacts by these interdependent communities they adopted common defense and foreign policies, gradually consolidating politically in the early [[Volisanya Boba]] period. These highly decentralized "states" were linked only through their ethnolinguistic closeness, rather than mutual political allegiance.

As a result of decades of sustained internal migration, [[Immigration to Balisca|immigration from overseas]], and the [[Cosmocazo|emergence]] of a [[Baliscans|Baliscan identity]], these nationalist divisions gradually [[W:post-nationalism|lost importance]] as society transitioned became increasingly [[Cosmocazo|cosmopolitan]]. The political power of these states were eventually [[1883 Confederal Convention|abolished]] in favor of [[W:Cantonalism|cantonalism]] after the [[Cantonal War]].

Until 1883, the loosely coupled confederation did not have a central political organization. Issues thought to affect the whole Confederation were the subject of periodic meetings in various locations. The Articles of Confederation (1810) provided few details concerning institutions, and this was not discussed until the [[1826 Confederal Convention]] in Alessandria, but was largely ineffective, as all [[Bayara]]n regions except [[Carpinchera Republic|Carpinchera]] boycotted the congress. The [[Baliscan Civil War|Bayaran secession]] and mobilization against the Congress led to an early termination without an definitive agreement. Around the same time, Castejón was considered to be the {{W|frontier}} of the fledgling confederation, a settlement in the "middle ground" in between cultures, peoples, and beyond state control. It gradually came to be called the "place of no god", as it was suspiciously viewed as an aberration by many both before and after independence due to its flamboyant and cosmopolitan culture. Bayaran armies conducted an ill-fated campaign to capture [[Alpujarra]] in 1827, which culminated in the [[Siege of Castejón]] and saw the intervention of the [[Maroon Confederacy]] on the side of the Confederalists.

After years of debate, shifting demographics, and negotiations and [[Concordia Accords|reconciliation]] between the confederations of Indigenous peoples, [[Maroon Confederacy|Araucaria]], and the [[republics of Balisca|communities of Balisca]], the [[1883 Confederal Convention]] symbolical recognized Castejón as the "cradle of the confederation", establishing the city as the ''de jure'' capital. As a prototypical confederal compromise, the Convention agreed to the location of other administrative institutions, such as the Polytechnical School (1854) to a diverse variety of communes across the Confederation, which was later ratified by the cantons.{{collapse top|bg=#ccccff|title=Regions of Balisca}}
