=== Internal nationalism ===
{{Main|Internal nationalism in Balisca}}

Within Balisca, "internal nationalism" is a complex and evolving concept, differing significantly from traditional nation-state nationalism, and pertains to the distinct identities of its seven constituent communities. In the initial stages of the Confederation, "internal nationalism" was more pronounced, closely resembling ethno-linguistic or regional patriotism. The founding republics were described as "highly decentralized 'states' linked only through their ethnolinguistic closeness, rather than mutual political allegiance".

Despite these internal differences, these early nationalisms were contained by a shared anti-colonial stance and the necessity for mutual defense, fostering strategic alliances like the [[Hesperian League]] and the broader [[Northern Confederation]]'s common defense policies. These distinct "nationalisms" would inevitably lead to internal conflicts, such as the [[Pastry War]], the [[Xunta Nacionalista|Bayaran secession]] and the subsequent [[Baliscan Civil War]], where Bayaran nationalists attempted to restore and reinforce the republic's specific social and economic order. 

The [[1883 Confederal Convention]], which established Balisca as the "first 'cosmopolitan state'", and especially the [[Baliscan Revolution]] (1918-1921), fundamentally reshaped "internal nationalism." The Revolution "abolished" the political power of these former "states" in favor of radical [[W:Decentralization|decentralization]], transforming their identities into something distinct from traditional state-seeking nationalism. "Internal nationalism" has since then taken the form of strong regional or ethno-cultural pride and identity preservation, rather than a desire for separate political statehood. This manifests predominately as linguistic and cultural affirmations, in which each community celebrates its unique languages (e.g., [[W:Catalan language|Volisanian]] and [[W:Occitan language|Beron]] in [[Cerdanya]], Indigenous languages in [[Pindorama]], [[Mozbayani]] in [[Mozbayana|communities with that heritage]]), distinct historical narratives, traditional dress, cuisine, and local customs, which are sources of intense local pride.

===International relations===
{{Main|International policy of Balisca|International relations of Balisca}}

Balisca's long standing international policy has been developed on the basis of the [[Neutrality Acts]] (1951, 1992 and 2022), which establishes {{W|pacificism}} (not to be confused with {{W|pacifism}}), [[W:Defensivism|defensivism]], support for {{W|self-determination}}, {{W|Internationalism|international cooperation}} and the peaceful settlement of conflicts as the guiding principles of Balisca's relationship with other countries and multilateral organizations. International policy is handled by the [[Secretariat of International Affairs and Cooperation (Balisca)|Secretariat of International Affairs and Cooperation]] (''Secretaria d'Asuntos Internacionales y Cooperación''; SAIC) 

The Confederal Council has ultimate authority over foreign policy, tasked with reviewing and considering all diplomatic nominations and international treaties, as well as legislation relating to Baliscan overseas policy. Balisca's international policy is a by-product of the country's role in the [[W:Non-Alignment Movement|Non-Alignment Movement]] during the Cold War, the sole [[w:Developed country|advanced economy]] in Abyala, and a "[[W:Soft power|soft power]] superpower". Baliscan foreign policy has generally been based on the principles of {{W|internationalism}} and {{W|multilateralism}}, peaceful dispute settlement, and {{W|non-interventionism|non-intervention}} in the affairs of other countries. Historically, Balisca has largely remained neutral in international conflicts, with the exception of the [[Great Adonian War]].

Balisca's early period of neutrality was challenged by [[Potocsí]] as part of the [[Marañón dispute]] and mutual animosity dating back to the [[Volisanya Boba|''Volisanya Boba'']] period of the 18th century. Potocsí would later invade [[Pindorama]] in 1976, sparking the [[Camboriú War]]. Meanwhile, Balisca and [[Asikyira]] have avoided direct armed conflict, despite several periods of tense relations. This dates back to the waning years of the [[Aethiopian slave trade]]. Balisca is founding member of several international organizations, most notably the [[Adonian Community]], the [[COCO|Concepción Organization of Cooperation]], the [[Community of Abyalan States]] and [[w:OPANAL|OPANAL]]. In 2018, Balisca contributed over 420 million dollars to the Adonian Community regular budget.[[File:VSB Merced.png|thumb|The [[W:Hospital ship|hospital ship]] [[VSB Merced|''Merced'']] en route to [[East Fosia]] following the [[2012 Great Morsco earthquake]]]]
An increasingly prominent aspect of Balisca's internationalist policy is promotion of decentralized (non-state) [[W:Mutual aid|mutual aid]] to {{W|social development}} assistance [[W:Direct action|directly]] to vulnerable communities globally through [[W:self-managed social centers|autonomous social initiatives]]. Internationalist efforts include expanding access to [[W:Cuban medical internationalism|medical assistance]] through autonomous efforts, education, cultural exchange, [[W:Climate resilience|climate resilience]] and adaptation. This has largely manifested as a quiet and non-confrontational diplomacy based on [[W:Global citizens movement|global citizenship]] and [[W:Global justice movement|global justice]]. 

Total aid in 2018 was estimated to be around {{I$|link=yes}}13 billion per year that includes: technical cooperation of around {{I$|link=yes}}5.1 billion ({{I$|link=yes}}2.2 billion in 2018 provided directly by International Secretariat), and an estimated {{I$|link=yes}}950 million for in-kind expertise provided by Baliscan institutions specializing in technical cooperation. Balisca is the largest contributor to the [[International Food Assistance Program]] ({{I$|link=yes}}2.0 billion). This is in addition to humanitarian assistance and contributions to multilateral development agencies.

In the early years of the {{W|Cold War}}, Balisca made a major effort to promote {{W|solidarity}} within the world's nonalignment movement, which represented over 90 nations. Apart from humanitarian interventions in revolutionary conflicts and civil wars, Balisca made world-wide commitments to social-and economic programs in 40 poor countries. This was made possible by the rapidly improving Baliscan economy in the 1960s and 1970s. The largest programs involved major construction projects, in which over 40,000 Baliscans provided technical advice, planning, and training of engineers. Educational programs involved 50,500 teachers. In addition thousands of specialists, technicians, and engineers were sent as advisors to agricultural mining and transportation sectors around the globe. Balisca hosted 100,000 international students, chiefly from Africa and Latin America, in health programs and technical schools. Balisca's extensive program of social and medical support to the {{W|Global South}} achieved widespread international attention. 

Balisca historically claimed {{convert|5,053,735.3|km2|abbr=on}} of [[Baliscan Arktici|Arktici]], where it has had the world's oldest [[Punto Baliscano|continuous presence]], since 1882, though all such claims fall under the provisions of the 1961 [[Arktici Treaty]], of which Balisca is a founding signatory.

===Military===
{{Main|Confederal Armed Forces}}

[[File:Confederal militia.png|thumb|330x330px|[[Autonomous Militia|Autonomous militia]] (''milicias  autónomas'') are the decentralized {{W|home defense}} forces, organized at the [[Cantons of Balisca|cantonal]] level.]]
The Confederal Armed Forces (FAC) are the military forces of the Confederation. They are focused on defending Balisca's borders, sovereignty, and citizens. Spending on Balisca's defense has significantly decreased since the end of the {{W|Cold War}}. The Baliscan military was most recently engaged in the {{W|2021 Kabul airlift}}. The nation's military relies heavily on it's large stockpile of [[W:sufrace-to-air missiles|surface-to-air]] and [[W:Anti-ship missile|anti-ship missiles,]] along with a relatively new [[Baliscan Air Grid|satellite/air defense network]]. Balisca utilizes {{W|high-tech weapons systems}} designed and manufactured domestically and in other nations, such as [[Burawa]]. Balisca's defense is based on an adherence to [[W:pacificism|pacificism]] coordinated at the confederal level through the [[Defense Council (Balisca)|Defense Council]], and there is public oversight of the Army, the Navy, the Aerospace Force, but not the [[Autonomous Militia]]. The secondary missions include committing to multinational operations within the framework of the [[Adonian Community]].
[[File:Baliscan Sukhoi Su-30 MkII.png|left|thumb|279x279px|A Baliscan [[W:Sukhoi Su-30MKK|Auster AU-30MKK]] {{W|Strike fighter|strike fighter}} developed by the [[Auster|Auster Corporation]].]]
The enlistment is age between 18 and 35 years old. Real military expenditures have steadily decreased year on year since 1985, and the military budget in 2023 was 1.0% of GDP, a historical low.

The country had a historically weak domestic arms industry, and up until the [[Camboriú War]] it largely relied on foreign sources of arms. Post-Camboriú War saw the development of a domestic arms industry with [[Balisca-Burawa relations|Burawi assistance]] aimed at bolstering the confederal policy of [[W:armed neutrality|armed neutrality]]. Balisca has restrictive arms export controls that exclude nations currently under {{W|international sanctions}}, those in {{W|conflict zone|active conflict zones}}, and those found to be committing {{W|human rights abuses}}. As such, it accounts for less than 1% of worldwide weapons sales, and exports arms to less than 10 countries. Conversely, Balisca was ranked the as one of the world's smallest major arms consumers by the United Nations in 2015. It maintained a long-standing policy of prohibiting the export of advanced arms and other other secret technologies for concerns that they could be replicated or copied. These restrictions were relaxed by the government of [[Cristiano Sandoval]] in 2007, leading to the first [[Salto AMCA MK.1|joint partnership]] between a foreign ([[Burawa National Defense Corporation|Burawi]]) and [[Auster|Baliscan]] arms company in 2016.

The armed forces have been surrounded with controversy since the founding of the Confederation, as their centralization under confederal administration is only authorized during a period of a [[confederal emergency]], an issue or event that is determined to require [[W:Collective security|collective cooperation and organization at the confederal level]]. There have been x confederal emergencies since the 1808 [[Proclamation of Alcazaba]], most recently in 2001. The confederation declared an "indefinite" [[W:Climate emergency declaration|climate emergency]] in 1982. The debate intensified during the administration of [[Cristiano Sandoval]], which argued that worsening [[W:climate change|climate change]] constituted a confederal emergency and justified the composition of a confederal defense force capable of responding to the [[W:Effects of climate change|effects of climate change]]. Others have rejected this, arguing that the increasing sophistication of confederal forces contributes to the international [[W:Arms industry|arms industry]] and the [[W:Militarization|militarization of Balisca]], the latter of which could be weaponized against the [[Cantons of Balisca|cantons]] and [[Communes of Balisca|communes]], and potentially violate constitutional protections.
[[File:Arruazu class carriers.png|thumb|300x300px|The Arruazu-class {{W|helicopter carrier}}s of the [[Baliscan Navy|Confederal Navy]], docked at [[Naval Station Arayamar]]. ]]
Balisca's navy, the second-largest in the Algarbes, has continuously operated some of the most powerful warships in the Northern and Western hemispheres as a result of a [[Abyalan dreadnought race]] between itself, [[Asikyira]], and [[Krauseland]]. Today, it has a group of specialized elite in retaking ships and naval facilities, protecting islands and isolated areas of Baliscan interest, and, controversially, the demonstrated ability to seize [[W:Oil platforms|oil platforms]]. It's the only navy in the region that operates an {{W|aircraft carrier}}, additionally being one of twelve navies in the world to operate them. Balisca's navy is considered to be a capable and agile "multi-regional {{W|blue-water navy}}".

Balisca does not possess {{W|nuclear weapons|nuclear}}, {{W|biological weapons|biological}}, or {{W|chemical weapons|chemical}} {{W|weapons of mass destruction}}, and has committed itself to a maintaining a nuclear-free Abyala. Balisca maintained an [[Balisca and weapons of mass destruction|secret nuclear program]] out of fears of [[Markland and weapons of mass destruction|Markland's own program]] throughout the 1970s, but ended it's program and destroyed its research in 1987 prior to its ratification of the [[Treaty of x]]. Additionally, Balisca has signed the [[Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons]], and ratified the [[Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons]].

===Human rights===
{{Main|Human rights in Balisca}}

Human rights in Balisca enjoy a high level of protection, both in theory and in practice, and are enshrined in the [[Constitution of Balisca|Constitution of the Confederation]], specifically by the [[Basic Law: Human Dignity and Liberty]]. The country has ratified most international human rights treaties. Reports from independent organizations such as [[w:Amnesty International|Amnesty International]] certify a high level of compliance with human rights, while still pointing out several issues, in particular {{W|police brutality}} and mistreatment of [[w:refugees|refugees]]. The 2018 [[w:Freedom in the World|Freedom in the World]] report by US-funded [[w:Freedom House|Freedom House]] gives Balisca a score of "1" (the best possible) for both {{W|political rights}} and {{W|civil liberties}} for the eighth year in a row.

Balisca has signed and ratified most of the international human rights treaties without reservations, including:

* [[w:International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights|International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights]] (ICCPR)  
* [[w:International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights|International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights]] (ICESCR) 
* [[w:International Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Racial Discrimination|International Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Racial Discrimination]] (CERD)
* {{W|Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples}} (UNDRIP)
* [[w:Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination against Women|Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination against Women]] (CEDAW)
* [[w:Convention against Torture|Convention against Torture]] (CAT)

Balisca has also ratified both Optional Protocols to the [[w:International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights|ICCPR]]. Balisca is subject to the [[w:Universal Periodic Review|Universal Periodic Review]] process as it is a [[Adonian Community|member state of the Adonian Community]]. It has completed two rounds of the Universal Periodic Review, the latest in 2013. Balisca has not recognised the jurisdiction of the [[w:Inter-American Court of Human Rights|Inter-American Court of Human Rights]], due to its rejection of the {{W|American Convention on Human Rights}}. Balisca did at one point seriously consider ratification, but has decided against it, despite being in principle in favor of such a treaty. The ACHR, having been largely drafted by the predominantly {{W|Roman Catholic}} nations of Latin America, contains anti-abortion provisions, specifically, Article 4.1:

{{quote|Every person has the right to have his life respected. This right shall be protected by law and, in general, from the moment of conception. No one shall be arbitrarily deprived of his life.}}

This conflicts with the current [[Abortion in Balisca|legality of abortions in Balisca]]. Although Balisca could ratify the convention with a reservation with respect to abortion, that would contradict Balisca's stated opposition to the making of reservations to human rights treaties. Another solution would be for the other states to remove the anti-abortion provisions, but that is unlikely to occur due to strong opposition to abortion in those countries.

====Indigenous rights====
{{Main|Indigenous peoples in Balisca|Indigenous nations in Balisca}}

Significant efforts have been (and continue to be) made to restore [[W:Indigenous sovereignty|Indigenous sovereignty]]. Early history efforts centered on the [[Treaty of Yvyra'ara]], and intensifying with the [[1883 Confederal Convention]]. Current efforts focus on achieving {{W|social justice}}, and continued facilitation of {{W|Indigenous decolonization}}. Traditional Indigenous cultures have enjoyed significant revivals across Balisca, which was further bolstered by the [[Baliscan Revolution]]. A number of socio-economic initiatives have been instigated with the aim of "closing the gaps" and aiding the restoration of indigenous sovereignty. Political and economic redress for historical grievances is also ongoing (see [[Indigenous nations in Balisca]] and [[Reconciliation council (Balisca)|reconciliation councils]]).

The bulk of contemporary Indigenous peoples in Balisca mostly reside both in their traditional highland settlements as well as increasingly in Balisca's urban areas. There are also the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|plains indigenous peoples]], which have always lived in the lowland areas of the island. Ever since the independence of Balisca, armed resistance and other efforts have been under way in indigenous communities to protect and revive traditional cultural practices and preserve their distinct traditional languages. In addition, several indigenous communities have become extensively involved in the {{W|homesteading}}, {{W|sustainable agriculture}} and {{W|ecotourism}} industries with the goal of achieving increased {{W|economic self-reliance}} and maintaining {{W|cultural integration}}.
====LGBTQ rights====
{{Main|LGBTQ rights in Balisca}}

{{See also|Baliscan values}}[[File:Bathers at San Niccolo.jpg|thumb|260x260px|[[W:Hammam|''Hamán'']] ''en Castejón,'' {{Circa|1670}} provides a glimpse into the long history of [[W:Non-heterosexual|non-heteronormative]] and [[W:Homoeroticism|homoerotic]] culture in the city, dating back to the 17th century.]]Widespread acceptance and support for LGBTQ inclusion has been regarded as some of the [[LGBTQ rights in Abyala|most progressive in Abyala]], and the world. Balisca has long been recognized as one of the most LGBTQ-friendly countries in the world and LGBTQ culture has had a significant role in the development of Baliscan literature, music, cinema and other forms of entertainment as well as social issues and politics. Tolerance of [[W:Non-heterosexual|non-heteronormative]] sexual activity was relatively common historically as a result of early [[W:Ethnoreligious|ethnoreligious]] [[W:Pluralism|pluralism]] (especially among [[Catharism|Cathars]]), but it was not until the 1808 [[Proclamations of Alcazaba|Proclamations of Alcabaza]] that same-sexual activity was explicitly legal. An age of consent equal with that of heterosexual activity was established in 1810. Rapid progress was made regarding LGBTQ rights in Balisca following the [[Baliscan Revolution|social revolution]] that swept the confederation in 1919. Castejón became the first Baliscan territory to prohibit the practice of [[W:conversion therapy|conversion therapy]] in 1946, while Balisca as a whole became the first country to fully do so in 1948.
[[File:Día Internacional de la Memoria Transgénero - Merced.png|left|thumb|The [[W:Transgender Pride flag|Transgender Pride flag]] hoisted at the [[Merced|Cabildo of Merced]] during the 2024 [[W:Transgender Day of Remembrance|Transgender Day of Remembrance]].]]
Balisca also has the world's most comprehensive {{W|transgender rights|transgender recognition}} and protections; regional and confederal policies protect the right of self-determination and [[W:Self-identification|self-identification]], allowing people to determine their [[W:Gender identity|gender identity]] without facing barriers such as [[W:Hormone therapy|hormone therapy]], surgery or psychiatric diagnosis that labels them as having an abnormality. Because of this prevailing attitude, as well as the creation of alternative schools and the first transgender [[W:Self-managed social center|self-managed community centers]], Balisca has been considered to be the trailblazer for the "trans revolution" in the world. Since 2015, the [[W:World Health Organization|World Health Organization]] has cited Balisca as an exemplary country for protecting transgender rights. On 16 October 1970, the electorate approved the addition of protections for "[[W:Gender expression|gender identity and expression]]" to the constitution via referendum.  

The country's capital and largest city, Castejón, has been an important recipient of {{W|LGBT tourism|LGBTQ tourism}} since the 1880s and has been described as "Abyala's {{W|gay capital|queer capital}}". Compared to its Abyalan neighbors, Balisca provides significantly more resources for LGBTQ people facing persecution in their home countries, and it accepts LGBTQ asylum applicants. Unlike other countries that accept [[W:LGBTQ migration|LGBTQ migrants]], in Balisca they disperse across the entirety of the territory. This phenomenon dates back to 1975, when Baliscan immigration law recognizing sexual persecution as grounds for seeking asylum. As of 2008, Balisca is one of only five nations (along with [[Sanyan]], [[Acadia]], [[Ayorin]], and [[tba]]) to have enacted immigration equality allowing for partner sponsorship.

