The economy of Balisca is the largest in both [[Abyala]] and the Northern Hemisphere. As of 1 April 2023, Balisca's gross domestic product (PPP) amounts to {{INTL$|link=yes}}6.187 trillion. The country's GDP grew 4.5% in 2022, after contracting 3.3% in 2021. Balisca is responsible for around 20% of the economic activity in Abyala, 16% of its exports, and 16% of its imports. A member of the {{W|G8}} and the {{W|G20|Group of 20}} leading industrialized countries, it is ranked as the world's fifth largest economy by both {{W|purchasing power parity}} and {{W|Nominal GDP|nominal}} measurements. Balisca is a founding member of the [[COCO|Concepción Organization of Cooperation]] (COCO), an {{W|economic union}} committed to the establishment of an international cooperative system as an {{W|alter-globalization|alternative}} to the current system of {{W|corporate globalization}}, through the implementation of {{W|cooperative economics|cooperative economic ideals}}, such as [[W:Solidarity economy|economic solidarity]], {{W|mutualism (economic theory)|mutualism}}, {{W|economic democracy}} and {{W|global justice movement|global justice}}.

The Baliscan currency is the [[Baliscan sol]] (S/). Balisca is one of Abyala's most stable and prosperous nations, and is one of the leading nations in human development, income equality, [[social Development Index|social development]], globalization, and low perception of corruption. Balisca tops lists in the region in regards to HDI and {{W|inequality-adjusted Human Development Index|inequality-adjusted HDI}}, based on its economy and regulatory environment, earning .913 and .889 respectfully, placing first out of all countries listed by a wide margin. Balisca has been considered by the World Bank as a [[w:High-income economy|"high-income economy"]] since July 1964. In 2022, around 1,67% of the population was estimated to live on less than {{I$}}45 (the national {{W|poverty line}}) a day.

Balisca has one of the highest degrees of {{W|Economic freedom#Socialist viewpoints|economic freedom}} of all Abyalan countries and [[Index of Economic Freedom#2025 Index|ranking 17th worldwide]] (just above [[x]]), owing to its [[W:Circular economy|circular]] {{W|Doughnut (economic model)|doughnut economic model}} which strives towards {{W|Right to an adequate standard of living|universal access to a just standard of living}}. This has resulted in an excess of regulation that heavily discourages the presence of {{W|multinational corporation|multinational}} corporations in favor of small and medium sized cooperatives, which has also translated into lower incoming investment. Balisca is considered the "land of {{W|cooperative}}s" with the twenty largest {{W|cooperative federation}}s accounting for around 5% of GDP in 2022.

Balisca's period of growth-oriented policies were halted permanently by the {{W|1973 oil crisis}}, [[October 76|civil unrest]], [[Camboriú War|conflict with Potocsí]] and the [[Special Period|sustained period]] of {{W|energy crisis|energy}}, {{W|food crisis|food}} and {{W|economic crisis|economic crises}} leading to sustained {{W|economic degrowth}}. The period radically transformed Baliscan society and the economy, as it necessitated the revival of [[rural society in Balisca|rural Balisca]], hastened the shift to {{W|organic agriculture}}, curtailed meat consumption, decreased use of automobiles, and overhauled [[transport in Balisca|transport]], health, and diet countrywide. However, Balisca made significant strides in reducing dependency on foreign sources for fuel through {{W|ethanol production}}, increased {{W|energy efficiency}}, {{W|renewable energy}} proliferation, and the country went on to exit the {{W|economic depression}} in 1983.

Confederal-level efforts to revive economic growth met with minimal success and were further hampered by the {{W|Early 2000s recession|global slowdown leading into 2000}}. Fortunately, its economy remained resilient because of its relatively decentralized structure based on regional and cantonal {{W|worker cooperative}}s and {{W|self-employment}}, and the country was ultimately one of the few to be less affected by the {{W|Great Recession}}. It still suffered from a significant loss of multinational business and investor confidence. By the start of the {{W|2010s}}, Balisca had embraced limited economic reforms, and growth accelerated to around 4.0% in 2014, before stabilizing and decelerating to 1.8, 1.5, and 1.3 percent growth over the ensuing three years. 

===Sectors===
{{Main|Agriculture, forestry, and fishing in Balisca|Mining in Balisca}}

[[File:Volcanic Landscapes of La Gomera 14.jpg|left|thumb|300x300px|[[W:Terrace (earthworks)|Terraced fields]] of the agricultural village of [[Carita Linda]] ([[Basilisco]])]]
Agriculture is an important and increasingly innovating component of Balisca's economy as crops provide a significant percentage of the nation's domestic food needs. Along with allied sectors like {{W|mariculture}}, {{W|pisciculture}} and fishing accounted for 11.3% of the GDP in 2023. Balisca is also one of the largest producers of various agricultural products, and also has a large cooperative network that proves around 60% of the food in Balisca. These cooperatives are typically oriented towards meeting domestic demand, rather than for export, and this is reflected in strict restrictions of {{W|agribusiness}} and protectionist policies. 
[[File:2019-03-16 01 Aquaculture in Chile.jpg|thumb|420x420px|Aquaculture fish farming in the [[Strait of Ivorra|Estero de Ivorra]] inlet of [[Arcadia|Arcadia island]].]]
Among the aquacultures practiced in Balisca, [[W:Atlantic salmon|Ethiopian salmon]] is by far the largest sector. Apart from salmon and trout, Baliscan aquaculture also produces [[W:Turbot|turbots]] and [[W:Mollusc|molluscs]], in particular ''[[W:Mytilus platensis|Mytilus platensis]],''  [[W:Placopecten magellanicus|''Placopecten magellanicus'']], [[W:Pacific oyster|Shendan oysters]], ''[[W:Aulacomya ater|Aulacomya ater]]'' (a giant mussel), [[W:Red abalone|red abalone]], and [[W:Eastern oyster|Eastern oyster]]. In terms of [[W:Seaweed farming|seaweed farming]], ''[[W:Macrocystis|Macrocystis]]'' is cultivated and harvested.

The main areas of aquaculture in Balisca lie in the northern half of the country in particular in the interior waters of [[Gran País del Sur]] and to a lesser extent [[Piscaria]] and the [[W:Fjord|fjords]] and channels of the southern [[Willi Mapu]] territory.

Beyond this, agriculture in Balisca is threatened by {{W|climate change}} and restricted by [[Constitution of Balisca|constitutionally-imposed]] environmental protections which have to a degree restricted agricultural growth, while also leading the field to adapt and innovate, embracing {{W|composting}}, {{W|urban agriculture}} and {{W|agricultural cooperatives}}. Beginning with crises of the 1970s, agriculture in Balisca began to diversify beyond the traditional products, with many food-based oils and specialty crops are now raised. Some of Balisca's main agricultural products include {{W|oat}}, sunflower seeds, lemons, soybeans, grapes, corn, cannabis and hemp, peanuts, tea, and wheat. {{W|Commercial fishing}} has declined significantly in recent years as stronger environmental protections adopted by the regions have limited stocks of the most popular catches, and {{W|commercial whaling}} was banned in 1985.
[[File:Baliscan cannabis grower.jpg|thumb|A cannabis grower in western [[Vitacura]] posing with harvested plants. Balisca is the world's largest producer of cannabis.]]
Industry in Balisca is relatively specialized and driven by a {{W|Circular economy|circular model}} that maximizes {{W|resource recovery}}. The country has a long history of exporting significant amounts of manufactured or finished products overseas. Balisca's membership in the [[Abyalan Mutual Development Community|Abyalan Mutualist Community]] and [[COCO]] have been two of the main factors driving industrial exports as it has expanded access to existing markets and opened new markets. Industry accounts for 29 percent of GDP. As a result of economic problems in other Abyalan countries, industrial production growth declined by 7 percent in 1999, but rebounded in 2000 by posting a modest level of growth of 2 percent. Among the main industries in Balisca are biotech, automobile production, textiles, energy production, and construction. 

The [[W:Tertiary sector|tertiary sector]] (trade and services) represented 64.0% of the country's GDP in 2018, according to [[Instituto Confederal de Stadística]] (ICS). It covers a wide range of activities: commerce, accommodation and catering, transport, communications, financial services, real estate activities and services provided to businesses, public administration (urban cleaning, sanitation, etc.) and other services such as education, social and health services, research and development, sports activities, etc., since it consists of activities complementary to other sectors, such as the [[W:Quaternary sector of the economy|quaternary sector of the economy]], which in 2022 represented 13.8% of the confederation's total workforce.

====Technology====
{{Main|Science and technology in Balisca}}

Advancement in technology in pursuit of the {{W|knowledge society}} has been at the forefront of Baliscan economic and educational strategy since the [[Baliscan Revolution]]. Successive confederal governments since the revolution have made significant investments in scientific research grants with ε84 billion spent on average each year. In 2021, Balisca dedicated a record 5.1% of its GDP ({{INTL$|link=yes}}306.1 billion) to {{W|research and development}}, five times [[Baliscan Armed Forces|it's]] {{W|military budget}} that same year (1.0% of GDP, or {{INTL$|link=yes}}61.8 billion). In his 2022 New Year [[Cabildo Address]], Secretary General [[Zavier Bilal]] advocated for billions in additional funding to support the implementation of {{W|civic technology}}, embracing and expanding Balisca's {{W|civic hacking}}, {{W|artificial intelligence in government}}, and {{W|open-source governance|open-sourced governance}}. The confederation later adopted the [[Cybersecurity and Privacy Initiative]] of 2022, which expanded upon [[Basic Law: Protection of Privacy|Article XIII of the Basic Law]], which declares the {{W|right to privacy}} to be a basic right.

{{W|Research and development}} has been a main focus of the current Baliscan Innovation initiative, with grants to universities and their students making up nearly 50% of their technology investment budget. Top Baliscan schools have produced 8 [[wikipedia:Nobel Prize winners in Physics|Nobel Prize in Physics]], 3 [[wikipedia:Nobel Prize in Chemistry|Nobel Prize in Chemistry]] and 2 in [[wikipedia:Nobel Prize in Medicine|Medicine]]. In 2015 their universities had also produced 82,000 [[wikipedia:BSc|BSc students]]. Additionally, Balisca has been home to some famous inventors and engineers, including [[tba]], the inventor of [[x]], as well as [[tba]], the father of [[y]].
[[File:Miramar Space Launch Center.jpg|thumb|375x375px|[[AXA]]'s Itara X rocket, which was launched from the [[Maravíes Aerospace Center]], in [[Aprica]] as part of [[Project Luna Horizons]].]]
Balisca has also invested deeply into the development of {{W|supercomputers}}, which are consistently ranked near [[TOP500|the top]]. Since the 1990s much of the manufacturing industry has grown to utilize industrial [[wikipedia:robots|robots]], and Balisca has the fourth-highest density of industrial robots, at 380 robots per 10,000 workers.
[[File:BSCC factory.jpg|left|thumb|The largest semiconductor manufacturing facility in [[Silico]], [[Cerdanya]] of the [[Baliscan Semiconductor Corporation]] (BSC)]]
With the successful adoption of the 1988 [[Ethical Innovation Initiative]], Balisca significantly expanded its policy on the research and sale of technology developments to encourage {{W|biotechnology|bio}} and {{W|environmental biotechnology}} and attract new talent from around the world. The policy change prompted hundreds of startups to pop up in cities like [[Merced]] and [[Navassa]]. 

Balisca continues to rely heavily on its [[W:Technology|technology]] sector, a specialist in [[W:Manufacturing|manufacturing]] [[W:Outsourcing|outsourcing]]. [[W:LCD|LCD]] manufacturing and [[W:LED|LED]] lights are two newer sectors in which Baliscan companies are moving. [[W:Orchid|Ba]]<nowiki/>lisca’s information technology (IT) and electronics sector has been responsible for a vast supply of products since the 1980s. The [[Industrial Technology Research Institute]] (ITRI) was created in the 1973 to meet new demands from the burgeoning tech industry. This led to start-up companies like [[BSC|Baliscan Semiconductor Corporation]] (BSC) and the construction of the [[Silico Science Park|Silico Science and Industrial Park]] (SSP), which includes around 300 high-tech companies and 150,000 employees. It placed sixth in producing [[W:Thin-film-transistor liquid-crystal display|Transistor-Liquid Crystal Display]] (TFT-LCD panels) (? percent) and ninth for LCD monitors (? percent) and LED (? percent). Balisca is still somewhat reliant on offshore technologies, importing up to ε25 billion worth of machinery and electrical equipment from [[Burawa]], [[Okaiken]] and [[Zong]].

In fact, the TFT-LCD industry in Balisca grew primarily from confederal (state)-guided personnel recruitment and inter-firm technology diffusion to develop its own industry independent of the dominant global neoliberal system. This is due to Balisca’s unique trend of export-oriented small and medium enterprises (SME) – a direct result of domestic-market prioritization by [[W:State-owned enterprises|state-owned enterprises]] (SOE) in its formative years. The development of SMEs allowed better market adaptability and inter-firm partnerships, and eventually – alongside other than firms like [[W:Acer Inc.|Acer]] and [[W:Asus|Asus]] – expanded to original design manufacturing (OBM). 

In the contemporary world economy, Balisca is a global hub for {{W|exotechnology}}, is the second-largest exporter of {{W|information technology}} (behind India), and it has a prominent [[W:Semiconductor|semiconductor]] and electronics industry, controversially developed wholly by the state as an [[essential industry]].

Balisca was ranked 8th in the {{W|Global Innovation Index}} 2021, up from 11th in 2020 and 12th in 2019. Additionally, Balisca today is known as a launchpad of a mature {{W|mobile market}}, where developers can reap benefits of a market where very few technology constraints exist. There is a growing trend of inventions of new types of media or apps, utilizing the {{W|4G}} and {{W|5G}} internet infrastructure in Balisca. Today, the country's well developed infrastructure to meet a density of population and culture that has the capability to create strong local particularity. The tech industry's share of total Balisca exports (merchandise plus services) increased from less than 4% in FY1990 to about 25% in FY2020. The technologically-inclined services sector in Balisca accounts for 30% of the country's GDP and 30% of export earnings as of 2019, while employing only 25% of its workforce, according to ICS (2020). 

As of 2016, 68.6% of Baliscan households own a [[wikipedia:computer|computer]], and 87.8% own a {{W|Smart phone|mobile phone}}. By the 1990s, [[W:Computer science education|sustained government support]] for universal {{W|Technological literacy|technological}} and {{W|digital literacy}} and the increasing availability of technology and internet services sparked the first efforts to develop a [[W:Distributed search engine|distributed search engine]]. [[Nodos]], as it came to be known, was ultimately built utilizing significant portions of source code from the [[Asikyira|Asikyiran]] [[Limu]] search engine, through the efforts of [[COCO]]'s [[Cooperative Internet Federation]].

Balisca has embraced {{W|solar energy}}; its engineers are on the cutting edge of solar energy technology and its solar companies work on projects around the world. Over 80% of Baliscan homes use solar energy for hot water, one of the highest per capita in the world. According to government figures, the country saves around 20% of its electricity consumption per year because of its solar energy use in heating. Significant interest in renewables, specifically solar during the [[Special Period]] provided the conditions for what is an internationally renowned solar research and development industry in the country.
===Income, wealth, and poverty===
{{See also|Housing in Balisca|Baliscan housing shortage|Social protection in Balisca}}

In 2020, the {{W|Global Food Security Index}} ranked Balisca third in food security, giving the country a score of 81.2/100. Baliscans on average have around {{convert|70|m2|abbr=on}} of living space per dwelling, similar to that of a typical Sanese resident. For 2019, the [[Adonian Community Development Programme]] ranked Balisca 26th among 189 countries in its {{W|Human Development Index}} (HDI) and 14th among 151 countries in its {{W|inequality-adjusted HDI}} (IHDI).

There are no billionaires in Balisca. Prior to the 2019–2021 global SARS-CoV-2 pandemic, {{W|Credit Suisse}} listed some 250,000 Baliscan citizens as having a net worth in excess of ε1 million. The scope and strength of Balisca's {{W|Redistribution of income and wealth|redistributive economic system}} sprouted from [[W:Dual power#Modern usage by libertarian socialists|dual power]] movements and the eventually rise of the {{W|welfare state}} post-revolution are the reasons for Balisca's low levels of inequality. This [[W:Mutualism (economic theory)|mutualist system]], in particular, allows for negligible effects that market income inequality can have on "disposable income inequality (i.e. market income after taxes and transfers)". Balisca has seen economic inequality increase as a result of accelerating [[W:Centralism (Peru)|centralism]] (the consolidation of wealth and development to coastal urban centers) and thus neglecting [[Rural society in Balisca|rural Balisca]]. [[File:Alterra co-op.jpg|left|thumb|320x320px|''Alterra'', a {{W|social housing|cooperative housing}} and [[autonomous commune]] in [[Syracuse]] ([[Piedmont]])]]
[[File:Baccara towers.png|thumb|307x307px|[[W:housing estate|Public housing estates]] in the planned community [[Baccara]]]]
There were about 1.3 million inadequately sheltered or unsheltered persons (defined as sleeping in autonomous centers, encampments, shanty towns) in Balisca in January 2023, with almost two-thirds staying in an emergency shelter or transitional housing program. Migrants (to include refugees) are disproportionately affected by Balisca's [[Baliscan housing shortage|ongoing housing shortage]]: more than three-fourths of the 1.3 million are migrants. The phenomenon of unsheltered people seeking shelter or refuge in points of entry (such as airports and ports) has increased significantly since the beginning of the 21st century.

The Baliscan constitution includes, in [[Basic Law: Housing and Shelter|Article II of the Basic Laws]], a {{W|right to housing|universal right to housing}}, and as such {{W|public housing}} is one of the major housing policies of the government. Over eighty-five percent of Balisca's 145 million population lives in cooperative, public, or social housing.  

Forms of social housing include the cantonal-level [[Housing committee (Balisca)|housing committees]] and communal {{W|housing cooperative}}s. Almost all land in Balisca is communally-owned. The commune leases the right to use land for periods of time which vary based on use: industrial land can be leased for 30 years, commercial land for 40 years, and residential land for 70 years.

Traditionally, the right to residential land was sold at a discount while commercial and industrial real estate prices were determined by the market.

As of 2023, Balisca has the highest rate of [[W:Owner-occupancy|home ownership]] in the world. 92% of urban households own their home. In 2016, approximately 60 percent of immigrant households lived in PRH flats while 25 percent resided in subsidized sale flats (of all types).

Large-scale resettlement estates were built throughout the 1950s and 1960s as part of the ambitious {{W|urban redevelopment}} plans championed by [[Alvaro Casadesús]] and the [[Accelerationist International]] in the aftermath of the [[Baliscan Revolution]]. In 1963, the government of [[Mário Sá Justino]] launched the Ten-Year Housing Programme, which aimed to construct enough "satisfactory housing" to house an additional 5 million people within a decade. The confederation also adopted the Social Housing Scheme (SHS) in 1976 to enable lower-income households to purchase homes more easily. Public housing remains a key concern at all levels of Balisca's, with construction policies coordinated at the confederal level. There are current plans to construct some 550,000 units per year between 2022 and 2032. In 2022, housing construction in Balisca overwhelmingly (76%) consisted of {{W|infill}} development.

As of June 2023, 2,828,693 people, roughly 0.6% of the Baliscan population, were living in poverty, including 10,000 children, an overall decrease of 32% from 2010 levels. Of those impoverished, 150,000 live in deep poverty (family income below one-half of the poverty threshold). In 2017, the Baliscan territories with the lowest and highest poverty rates were [[Cadaqués]] (0.1%) and [[Cap de Pingüins]] (6.5%), respectively.

===Labour===
{{See also|Trade unions in Balisca}}
Sometimes labelled a {{W|regenerative economics|regenerative economy}}, the specific model practiced within Balisca is characterized as a form of {{W|libertarian socialism}} paired with aspects of {{W|cooperative economics|cooperative}}, {{W|participatory economics|participatory}} and {{W|market economy|market economics}} that holds an ideological alignment to the principles of a {{W|market socialism|market socialist-economy}}. Nearly 80% of all industries within Balisca are categorized as being {{W|cooperative}}ly operated (either by {{W|worker cooperative|workers}}, {{W|consumer cooperative|consumers}}, etc) {{W|organizational self-management|managed by labor}}, where workers form {{W|worker cooperative}}s and participate in the direction of their respective industries. This dispersed {{W|social ownership}} has built a {{W|competitive market}}, where worker-collectives and labor unions seek to produce higher quality goods at a sustainable rate. Successive regional governments and {{W|worker cooperatives}} have steadily transitioned from a {{W|for-profit system}} to a domestic {{W|Regenerative economic theory|regenerative}} and {{W|post-consumerism|post-consumerist}} {{W|production for use}} system, but larger state owned entities have overwhelmingly continued to practice production models that place profiteering and international competition at the heart of production. Baliscan economists have agreed that this model of profiteering will remain necessary in the {{W|globalization|globalized system of capitalism}}.
[[File:Federación Anárquica del Trabajo poster.png|thumb|273x273px|1939 poster for the [[Federación Anárquica del Trabajo]] [[Trade unions in Balisca|trade union]]]]
{{W|Consumer spending}} is significantly lower in Balisca compared to other advanced economies, making up around 36% of the Baliscan economy. This reflects Balisca's [[W:Anti-consumerist|anti]] and [[W:Post-consumerism|post-consumerist]] orientation, it's library and [[W:Sharing economy|sharing economy]], the [[W:Right to repair|right to repair]], legislation against [[W: Planned obsolescence|planned obsolescence]], and a culture of [[W:Upcycling|upcycling]] and [[W:Recycling|recycling]] to reduce waste.

In 2025, the Baliscan labor force consisted of 87 million people. However, Balisca's employment rates for the {{W|working age population}} is one of the lowest of the OECD countries: in 2022, only 47.4% of the Baliscan working age population was in employment, compared to 63% in Volisania, 60% in Daras, and 59% in Breisland. This gap is due to the low employment rate for 16–24 years old group: 33% in 2012, compared to 47% in the OECD.

90% of the [[Trade unions in Balisca|Baliscan workforce is unionized]], compared to 81% in [[Burawa]], 46% in [[Volisania]], 30% in [[Asikyira]]. Depending on the worker-cooperative and labor union, the {{W|List of minimum annual leave by country|minimum annual leave with pay}} varies, although the average ranges between 40-45 days. All workers outside the realm of emergency services are guaranteed paid leave on a minimum of thirty days out of the year. {{W|Paid family leave}} is a universal right among the worker-cooperatives and parents are granted a stipend for their childbearing from the communal government. The [[Baliscan Selective Service]] has been a source for the nation's high {{W|workforce productivity}}, to which Balisca ranks 6th in the world, behind [[x]], [[x]], [[x]], [[x]] and [[Breisland]]. Baliscan workers rank seventh in productivity per hour. Workers in Balisca are documented as to having the highest rates of {{W|job satisfaction}}, with {{W|Job control (workplace)|job control}} and {{W|happiness at work|positive work atmospheres}} being the two main contributors. This has generated a healthy {{W|work–life balance}} with many Baliscans (including all public workers) working a {{W|four-day workweek|four-day, 24-hour workweek}}. 

These four-day workweeks are not based on a {{W|hourly wage}}, which the [[Cooperative Collective]] considers a form of {{W|wage slavery}}, it is instead based on a formula of {{W|salary|salaried wage}}, in which individuals working in cooperative enterprises earns according to their "share" of the enterprise, determined by hours worked, skill level, seniority. The "Equitable Society" compact, adopted in 1983 states that no share may be more than five times the smallest share. However, the all regions have adopted the [[Basic Pay Initiative]], guaranteeing a {{W|Living wage|minimum living wage}} for non-citizens working in the country. It is updated each quarter to keep pace with inflation, and all employers must meet to ensure a basic standard of living.

===Economic history and growth===
[[File:Chinese miners Idaho Springs.jpg|thumb|[[Jade Baliscans|Jade Baliscan]] miners in the [[Minera Alcocer S.A]] School of Mines' Experimental Mine near [[Sumacàrcer]], [[Cadaqués]], c. 1917.]]
Economic and social ideas brought on by the growing numbers of international immigrants attracted to the country during the gold boom (through the new [[Cerdanyan gold rush]] and the continuing [[Bayaran gold rush|Bayaran cycle]]) triggered a strong drive to industrialize and mechanize. Generous government sponsored-programs resulted in an [[Immigration to Balisca|unprecedented wave of migrants to Balisca]], resulting in significant [[Kaftia|Kaftian]] (particularly [[South Kaftia|South Kaftian]]) and [[Illypnia|Illypnian]] influence in Baliscan [[ethnography of Balisca|ethnography]] and socioeconomic policy.  

In the 1870s real wages in Balisca were around 86% relative to Burawa, rising to 98% in the first decade of the 20th century. GDP per capita rose from 55% of the [[Volisania|Volisanian]] average in 1880 to about 90% in 1905, similar to that of [[Sarta]], [[Xacan]] and [[Hersatia]]. Strong {{W|protectionism|protectionist trade policies}} in the 1880s prompted the rapid growth of a heavy industrial base, along with steel and iron production sites emerging in [[Cerdanya]] and [[Araucaria]], while [[Hesperia]] led Balisca’s textile, chemical, engineering and banking boom. However there was a disproportionate amount of investment allotted to Cerdanyan and Hesperian regions, compared to that of [[Bayara]] (due to its near-monopoly on Baliscan agriculture), and Araucaria due to exclusionary policies from both sides. This situation persisted in Bayara largely until the collapse of the coffee and sugar industry by the end of the 19th century. However, following the [[Baliscan Revolution]], the Bayaran regions rapidly caught up with the rest of the country. 

During the period between 1920 and 1930, Balisca experienced a second period of rapid [[W:Industrialization|industrialization]] and economic growth, this time driven by {{W|collective ownership}} and {{W|self-management}}. Baliscans, committed to principles of {{W|individual freedom}} and {{W|egalitarianism}}, established a decentralized economic system where workers had control over the {{W|means of productio}}n.

Under this {{W|libertarian socialist}} framework, factories, mines, and industries were organized into {{W|worker cooperative|worker-owned cooperatives}}. Decision-making was [[W:Decentralized planning|decentralized]], with workers collectively determining production processes, wages, and working conditions. This empowered the workforce and fostered a strong sense of ownership and responsibility, leading to increased productivity and efficiency.

Balisca’s industrialization efforts were marked by investments in infrastructure, technology, and education. The country witnessed the construction of railways, roads, and telegraph networks, facilitating the movement of goods and ideas. [[W:Universal access to education|Universal access to education]] was prioritized, with an emphasis on technical and [[W:Vocational training|vocational training]] to meet the growing demands of the industrial sector. Balisca’s rapid industrialization improved living standards, reduced inequality, and provided opportunities for [[W:Social mobility|social mobility]], while maintaining a commitment to [[W:Mutualism|mutualism]] and workers' self management. This model also extended to the political sphere, decentralizing economic policy to the respective cantons and regions, allowing for a wide variety of industries taking root across the country. At the same time, the aggressive regional action led to rapid development of hydroelectric infrastructure, railway networks, and the nation gradually armed itself. It around this time that the [[W:Aviation industry|aircraft manufacturing]] and [[W:Shipbuilding|shipbuilding]] industries first gained prominence. 
{{Further|Baliscan miracle}}[[File:Barrio Y 1920s.png|thumb|300x300px|[[Castejón]] reached two million inhabitants at the end of the 1920s, one of the largest cities in the [[Northern Hemisphere]]]]
The period from the end of the [[Great Adonian War]] to the early 1970s constituted a second era of accelerated economic growth. {{INTL$|link=yes}}88 billion in war bonds matured, and government policies financed a well-educated workforce. The demand for [[W:Consumer goods|consumer goods]] swelled, as did GDP and productivity. Fast economic expansion induced a massive [[W:Economic migrant|flow of migrants]] from rural Bayara to the industrial cities of the Cerdanya and Hesperia. Emigration was especially directed to the factories of the so-called "industrial square", the region placed between the major manufacturing centres of [[Concepción]], [[Merced]], and the seaport of [[Alessandria]]. Between 1955 and 1971, around 9 million people are estimated to have been involved in inter-regional migrations in Balisca, leading to population declines in [[Rural society in Balisca|the countryside]] and creating large [[Megaregions of Balisca|metropolitan areas]].
[[File:Alcántara Hesperides 1975.jpg|left|thumb|300x300px|[[Alcántara|Alcántara ''Hesperides'']] train on the ''[[Direto]]''. The development of the [[AVB]] revolutionized Baliscan transportation and signaled the decline of the {{W|airline industry}}.]]
The ''[[Direto]]'' Line became the inaugural section of the [[AVB|''Alta Velocidad Baliscana'']] (AVB) {{W|high speed rail}} service on 9 April 1969. The conventional Limited Express service took six hours and 40 minutes to cross the Baliscan Levante ([[Porciúncula]] to [[Jatiel]] via [[Castejón]]), but the AVB made the trip in just four hours, shortened to three hours and ten minutes by early 1973. It enabled day trips between Macaio and Castejón, two of the largest metropoli in Balisca, significantly changed the style of business and life of the Baliscan people, and increased new traffic demand. The service was an immediate success, reaching the 100 million passenger mark in less than three years on 24 July 1972, and one billion passengers in 1981.

At the same time, the doubling of Baliscan GDP between 1950 and 1962 had a massive impact on society and culture. Baliscan society was suddenly flooded with a huge variety of cheap consumer goods, such as automobiles, televisions and washing machines.  
[[File:October 73 riots.png|thumb|260x260px|{{W|Stagflation}} and the lackluster confederal response led to [[October 76|widespread civil unrest]], which coincided with the beginning of the [[Special Period]].]]
Balisca's period of growth-oriented policies were halted permanently by [[October 76|civil unrest]], [[Camboriú War|conflict with Potocsí]] and the [[Special Period|sustained period]] of {{W|energy crisis|energy}} and {{W|economic crisis|economic crises}} leading to sustained {{W|economic degrowth}}. The {{W|economic depression}} of this "[[Special Period]]" was at its most severe between 1977 and 1980. During this time {{W|food insecurity}} improved significantly thanks to expanded assistance from [[Burawa]], [[Dagit]] and members of the [[tba]], whom together transported over ? {{W|tonnes}} of food and other materials to Balisca during the Special Period. Changes during the Special Period included an unprecedented level of responsibility and cooperation at the confederal level by the communes and regions, and internationally, the [[Mutual Arms and Technology Agreement|diplomatic realignment]] of the country towards the [[Socialist Bloc]]. This period radically transformed Baliscan society and the economy, as it necessitated the revival of [[Rural society in Balisca|rural Balisca]], hastened the shift to [[W:Organic agriculture|organic agriculture]], curtailed meat consumption, permanently ended the growth of [[W:Car ownership|car ownership]] in the country as fuel was prioritized for diesel cargo trains and ships, and overhauled industry, health, and diet countrywide.

Today, Balisca is mainly characterized as having a [[W:Mutualism (economic theory)|mutualist economy]] based on {{W|state ownership|public}}, {{W|cooperative}}, and {{W|social ownership}} and is one of the leading examples of {{W|market socialism}}. The confederal administration still dominates in strategic "pillar" sectors such as energy production and heavy industries, and there is strong legislation discouraging the presence of [[W:Multinational corporation|multinational corporation]]<nowiki/>s in favor of [[W:Artisans|artisans]] and [[W:Worker cooperative|workers' cooperative]]<nowiki/>s. Cooperative enterprises have continuously expanded since the Baliscan Revolution, with around 6 million socially-run businesses recorded in 2018.

====Recent====
According to the Global Growth Generators index announced by [[El País]] in June 2022, Balisca currently has a low growth rating. It has high productivity and well-developed infrastructure, but has high labor costs and stringent economic, social and environmental policies that have caused it to gradually lose steam and made it unattractive to international investors. 
[[File:Palacio Cajal.png|thumb|280x280px|''[[W:L.O.V.E. (sculpture)|L.O.V.E.]]'' ("Freedom, Hate, Revenge and Eternity"), a [[W:Degrowth|degrowth]]-inspired {{W|public art|art installation}} in front of the [[Castejón Stock Exchange]]]]
The number of immigrants arriving in Balisca grew by over 70% between 1990 and 2020, largely attributed to increased instability in [[Western Fosia|Western]] and [[Central Fosia]], and [[Kaftia]] which has brought addition strain to Balisca's [[Baliscan housing crisis|sluggish housing market]]. Averaging an economic growth rate of 3.3% since 2014, Balisca has increased its hourly wage rates by a fifth within the first decade of the 21st century. During the next four decades, Baliscan GDP is expected to grow at an annualized average of 0.5%, a modest rate for an advanced economy until around 2050. The report highlights key factors for future growth: an aging working-age population supported by consistent immigration; growth in the manufacturing sector because of {{W|automation}} and increasing engineering skill levels; and the {{W|Degrowth|shrinking of the consumer market}} driven by a {{W|Post-consumerism|growing environmentalist consciousness}}.

Baliscan employment rates for the {{W|working age population}} is one of the lowest of the OECD countries: in 2021, only 47.4% of the Baliscan working age population was in employment, compared to 60% in [[Okaiken]], 59% in [[Breisland]], 49% in [[Andalus]], and the Baliscan employment rate was lower than that of most of Abyala. This gap is due to the low employment rate for 15–24 year olds: 38% in 2012, compared to 47% in the OECD. 

With the impact of the {{W|COVID-19 pandemic}} and [[2021 Baliscan civil unrest]], {{W|refusal of work}} has accelerated. Prevailing societal opinion supports gradual {{W|The Abolition of Work|abolition of work}}, which led to the introduction of the 24-hour workweek law in 2021. Between 2010 and 2012, the government attempted to combat unemployment with supply-side reforms, but was met with fierce resistance; these reforms were never implemented. Neoliberal economists attribute the low employment rate, particularly evident among {{W|young people}}, to high {{W|minimum wages}} that would prevent "low productivity" workers from easily entering the labour market, overly-generous [[Social protection in Balisca|government benefits and subsidies]], and widespread cultural {{W|Critique of work|criticism}} of the concept of work. Balisca has been leading the shift to {{W|automation}} of work, and has embraced the expansion of {{W|AI takeover|artificial intelligence}} in the workplace.

==Infrastructure==
===Energy===
{{Main|Energy in Balisca}}

{{See also|Transición energética|Green Protocol}}
[[File:Vitacura offshore wind farm.png|thumb|330x330px|[[Araucaria Eni|''Araucariano'' ''Eni 82'']] [[W:Offshore wind power|offshore wind farm]] on the coast of [[Vitacura]] ]]
In 2022, Balisca was the world's fourth-largest consumer of energy, consuming some 260.76 bn {{W|watts}} (about 1798.1 W per capita, making Balisca a {{W|2000-watt society}}), and 100% of its primary energy was domestically produced. In 2022, energy sources were: {{W|nuclear energy|nuclear}} (40.5%), {{W|wind energy|wind}} (29.8%) {{W|hydro-electric energy|hydro-electric}} (13.6%), {{W|solar energy|solar}} (14.5%), and other renewable sources (1.6%). In 1999 the government-led nuclear power industry announced plans for of a shift to {{W|thorium-based nuclear power}} for one-third of Balisca's reactors, alongside the phase out of the remaining two-thirds of {{W|uranium-based nuclear power}} plants by 2025 and a transition to renewable energy sources in their place. It also enforces energy conservation, green technologies, emission reduction activities, and aims to meet the country's electricity demands using 90% renewable sources by 2025. Balisca is fully committed to the [[Okami Protocol]], the [[Kuluba Agreement|Kuluba Agreement regarding climate change]], and several other treaties promoting biodiversity, low emission standards, water management, recycling, and the renewable energy commercialization.
[[File:Borgo Niru Power Plant.png|left|thumb|[[Borgu Niru Nuclear Power Station|Borgo Niru Nuclear Power Station]] on the coast of [[Sicania]]]]
[[File:Akutuku Solar Farm.jpg|thumb|330x330px|[[W:Photovoltaic power station|Photovoltaic energy farm]] in the [[Akûtukû Desert]]]]
The Baliscan push to achieve {{W|energy independence}} stemmed from the rise of {{W|green nationalism}} during and after the [[Camboriú War]] and simultaneous {{W|1973 oil crisis}}, intensifying significantly during the [[Special Period]]. Additionally, the Baliscan government sought to eliminate reliance on autocratic and authoritarian states for natural resources, thereby avoiding any conflicts with the [[Basic Law: Universal Humanism]] and embracing the {{W|soft energy path}}. Utilizing research from other nuclear power dependent nations such as [[Burawa]] and [[Okaiken]], Balisca rapidly developed the largest nuclear energy system in the [[Western Hemisphere|Western]] and [[Northern Hemisphere]], while significantly increasing funding for the development of {{W|renewable technology}} and sources of energy. This energy policy, called the the ''[[transición energética]]'' ("energy transition") significantly accelerated in the 1980s. This included a {{W|just transition}} for affected occupations, investments in wind and solar power, as well as incentives for individuals and businesses to install renewable energy systems, {{W|cogeneration}}, the development of {{W|mass transit}} to reduce the need for cars, and restrictions on {{W|air travel}} in support of [[AVB|high speed rail]]. One of the most notable developments in the transition to renewable energy in Balisca was the construction of the country's first large-scale wind farm in 1977. The farm, located on the coast, consisted of 50 wind turbines and had a total capacity of 25 megawatts. This was followed by several other wind farms in the 1980s and 1990s, and by the early 2000s, wind power accounted for a significant portion of Balisca's electricity generation. 

Since the 1950s, energy production has been gradually {{W|Soft energy path|decentralized}} to the communal level, although hydroelectric projects often require [[cantons of Balisca|cantonal-level cooperation]]. This "soft energy path" assumes that energy is but a means to social ends, and is not an end in itself. Soft energy paths involve efficient use of energy, diversity of energy production methods (matched in scale and quality to end uses), and special reliance on {{W|co-generation}} and "soft energy technologies" such as solar energy, wind energy, biofuels, {{W|geothermal energy}}, {{W|wave power}}, {{W|tidal power}}, etc.

The ''Transición energética'' is considered the cornerstone of the ongoing transition by Balisca to {{W|net zero}}, environmentally sound, reliable, and affordable energy supply.  The new system intends to rely heavily on renewable energy (particularly wind, photovoltaics, and hydroelectricity), energy efficiency, and energy demand management. Thanks to aggressive government action, all coal-fired generation plants in the country were closed before 2003, five years ahead of schedule, and the country reached 70% renewable energy production in 2015. All of Balisca's energy production became fossil fuel free in 2016, and it has remained this way since. Balisca became the first country in the world to {{W|Fossil fuel divestment|divest from fossil fuels}} in 2015. Legislative support for the ''Transición energética'' was significantly expanded in 2020 as part of a {{W|green recovery}} package in response to the {{W|COVID-19 pandemic}}, which included higher greenhouse gas (GHG) reduction goals of 80–95% by 2050 (relative to 1990) and a renewable energy target of 100% by 2030.

===Transportation===
{{Main|Transport in Balisca}}

{{See also|Low Impact Initiative}}[[File:LUMEN tunnel.png|thumb|The [[Confederal Cycling Network]] is the world's most extensive {{W|cycle infastructure|cycling network}} as of 2022, reaching all corners of Balisca.]]
[[File:Aerial view of Hokuto Chuo Interchange and its surrounding area - Nov 9, 2024.jpg|left|thumb|300x300px|Aerial photo of [[Sumacàrcer]] and its surroundings photographed from the north. The white line stretching straight from the upper left to the lower right is the [[Concepción–Merced high-speed rail line|HSR ''Mercepción'']]. The curved line below it is the [[Autopista of Balisca|Autopista 12]].]]
[[File:Navassa streetcar.png|thumb|300x300px|{{W|Active design}}, along with concepts such as the {{W|compact city|compact}}, {{W|healthy city|healthy}} and {{W|15-minute city}} have been implemented in urban areas, resulting in the vastly improved {{W|walkability}} and {{W|cyclability}} of Baliscan cities]]
In 2021, the transport sector in Balisca generated a turnover of about 219.4 billion sol, employing 3,935,700 persons in 253,700 enterprises. '''Rail is the backbone of transport in Balisca'''. The Baliscan rail network is noteworthy for its density, its coordination between services, its integration with other modes of transport, timeliness and a thriving domestic and [[Matarrañya|Cismatarrañya]] freight system. This is made necessary by strong regulations on [[W:Truck|truck]] transport, and is enabled by properly coordinated [[W:Intermodal freight transport|intermodal logistics]].

The crossing of the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] is an important route for Balisca transportation, as the mountains form a significant natural barrier between the constituent communities. Railway routes crossing the range began operations in 1916 with the [[Trans-Matarrañya Railroad]] with its central [[Basilicata Rail Tunnel]], followed in 1923 by the [[Jaraiz Tunnel]] and the [[Russafa Tunnel]] in 1937. As part of the [[New Railway Link (Balisca)|New Railway Link]] through the Matarrañya (NCF) in 2009 the [[Bubión Base Tunnel]] opened, and the [[Yabasa Tunnel]] opened on 2 November 2022. The national inland waterways network has a length of {{convert|800|km}} for commercial traffic in 2022.

Balisca has the largest railway system in Abyala, with 66,782 km (41,496 mi) of electrified lines in 2014, out of a full network of almost 88,000 km (29,826 mi). This system links all forty regions plus the Castejón Metropolis, and connects with [[Asikyira]] via the [[Baliscan-Asikyiran Friendship Bridge]]. Gauges are uniform throughout Balisca, and are harmonized with those of neighboring Siculia to maintain easy travel. Since the 1990s the system has been experiencing a greater degree of investment from the state and {{W|cooperative federation}}s, in both commuter rail lines and long distance lines, renewing rolling stock and infrastructure. In April 2008, by overwhelming majority the Baliscan Congress passed a law which prohibited the privatization of [[Baliscan Railways Group|Ferrocarriles Baliscana]], permanently ensuring public control on all of the country's railways. In 2017, Balisca's railway network carried 7.5 billion total passengers, the third-highest in the world, behind [[Peilan]] and [[Okaiken]]. Rail is the preferred mode of travel by 68% of Baliscans, one of the world's highest rates.

Communities both urban and rural across Balisca are heavily dependent on [[W:Public transit|public transit]]. Urban communes have extensive {{W|rapid transit}} networks, and has embraced the development of {{W|Transit-oriented development|transit-oriented}} {{W|transit village|villages}}, {{W|satellite cities}} and {{W|transit metropolis|transit metropoli}}. Additionally, Balisca has one of the most dense bicycle networks in the world. Cycling is a common mode of transport in Balisca, with 36% of Baliscan people listing the bicycle as their most frequent way of getting around on a typical day, as opposed to the public transport (45%) and private cars (11%). Cycling has a modal share of 27% of all trips (urban and rural) nationwide. In cities this is even higher, such as Yerba Buena which has 48%, and [[Navassa]] at 46%. This high frequency of bicycle travel is enabled by excellent {{W|cycling infrastructure}} such as {{W|cycle paths}}, {{W|cycle tracks}}, protected intersections, ample {{W|bicycle parking}} and by making cycling routes shorter and more direct (and therefore usually quicker) than cars.

High-speed rail service in Balisca was first introduced on 21 September 1969, with the completion of the ''[[Porciúncula–Jatiel high-speed rail line|Direto]]'' line, which revolutionized the way Baliscans travelled and found immediate success. Annual ridership has grown from 100 million in 1972 to 355 million in 2019, making the Baliscan high-speed rail network one of the most heavily used in the world. Balisca's high-speed rail network consists of upgraded conventional railways and newly built high-speed passenger designated lines (PDLs). All high-speed rail lines and rolling stock are state-owned and operated by the four companies of the [[Baliscan Railways Group]], the public operators of the [[AVB|AVB high-speed rail system]].

Balisca's high speed rail system's growth exploded in the beginning of the 21st century, through generous funding through the government's economic stimulus packages between 2005, 2010 and 2020. High speed rail has become the primary form of transportation pursued by the Baliscan government as it combats {{W|environmental degradation}} and seeks to eliminate dependency on cars. 
[[File:Las Vinas tubo.png|left|thumb|290x290px|The domestically produced and designed [[Alcántara|Alcántara ''Tubo'']] {{W|Electric multiple unit|EMU}} in [[Calatayud]], April 2024. It has a maximum speed of {{cvt|380|km/h|mph|abbr=on}}]]
[[File:Route 22 (Castejon) shield.svg|thumb|255x255px|A [[W:Highway shield|route shield]] for [[Autopista 22]] in the Castejón Metropolis]]
Since 1989, the [[Castejón|Castejón Metropolis]], all provincial capitals except [[Puerto Baliscano]], and all medium-sized towns were interconnected by {{convert|109412|km|0|abbr=on}} of paved roads, out of a total road network of {{convert|203374|km|0|abbr=on}}. Most important cities are linked by a shrinking number of controlled-access highways ('freeways'), limited-access roads ('expressways'), and highways. A single network of high-speed, divided, limited-access toll roads connects major cities, which are operated by {{W|toll-collecting enterprises}}. 

The sale of new {{W|Internal combustion engine|internal combustion vehicles}} was banned 2015, and they will be banned entirely in 2030. While {{W|electric cars}} are cheaper, they are still relatively expensive compared to other countries, thanks to government policies that heavily discourages increased car ownership. Additional car ownership fees are used to promote energy efficiency on electric cars. At just 26 percent of all distance traveled, car usage is the lowest of all G8 countries. Additionally, road transport (particularly in private vehicles) in Balisca is very expensive in international comparison, reflecting high financial burdens tied to car ownership (i.e. tolls and taxes). As a result of restrictive policies such as the prohibition of {{W|Parking minimums and maximums|parking maximums}} and size restrictions, cars purchased in Balisca are mostly [[w:A-segment|A-segment]] (called ''[[Baixo car|Baixo cars]]''; Baliscano for "short"), comparable to the adoption of [[w:Kei car|Kei cars]] in Okaiken.
[[File:Novalia Airport aerial.jpg|thumb|270x270px|[[Alessandria]]'s [[Novalia International Airport]] opened in 1996 and is currently one of the busiest airports in Balisca.]]
Regarding the confederal road network, in 2022 there were {{convert|368721|km}} of serviceable roads in Balisca, including {{convert|10812|km}} of {{W|motorways}} (called the [[Autopista of Balisca|''Autopista'']]), publicly-owned and cooperatively operated. In 2021, about 14,578,236 passenger cars (95 cars per 1,000 people) and 2,782,732 goods vehicles circulated on the confederal road network.

{{As of|2012|alt=In 2012}} there were about {{convert|18000|km|0|abbr=on}} of [[w:Waterway|waterway]]s, mostly comprising the [[Marañón River|Marañón]], [[Alagón (river)|Alagón]], [[Canyada river|Cañada]], [[Tapajós River|Tapajós]], and [[Py'aguasu]] rivers, with [[Yerba Buena]], [[Porciúncula]], [[Sagrajas]] and [[Uxiu]] being the most prominent [[w:Fluvial port|fluvial port]]s. There are some 322 ports in Balisca as of June 2022, with overlapping classifications of amongst them, some of which are multi-purpose, e.g. cargo, passenger, naval, and fishery. The five designated "super" container ports are Jatiel, Macaio, Alessandria, Concepción, and Xàbia. 23 are designated major/international, 125 designated as important, while there are also purely fisherman ports.  

In 2023, Balisca had the 7th largest passenger air market in the world with 62.4 million passengers. All of the world’s leading airlines fly into one of Balisca’s nineteen international airports, but a significant portion of passengers primarily fly into [[Concordia International Airport|Concordia Airport]], the busiest airport in Balisca, and [[Novalia International Airport|Alessandria Airport]], the second busiest airport in the country. Both airports are major hubs for trans-Ethiopian and transcontinental traffic. As of 2023 there were 183 airports with paved runways out of more than a two-thousand, down from 221 ten years ago. The [[W:Flag carrier|national airline]], [[Baliscana]] operates international flights to and from all of Balisca's major airports.

