In the [[2025 Baliscan census|2025 census]], Balisca had a total population of 214 million, of which 164 million are Baliscan nationals, with the remaining population being made up of foreign-born residents. In 2022, 92% of the total Baliscan population lived in cities, and 25.5% of all Baliscans lived in the [[Concordia]] conurbation. Balisca ranks first in the [[Western Hemisphere]] in total population and [[List of countries by population|sixth globally]]. Population density is of 30.63 persons per square kilometer of land area, well below the world average of 50 persons/km2. The population growth rate for 2020-2025 was an estimated 1.62% annually, with a birth rate of 17.7 live births per 1,000 inhabitants and a mortality rate of 3.1 deaths per 1,000 inhabitants. 

The main drivers of population growth are [[Immigration to Balisca|immigration]] and, to a lesser extent, [[W:Rate of natural increase|natural growth]]. Balisca has one of the highest per-capita immigration rates in the world, driven mainly by economic policy, {{W|free migration}} policies and also {{W|family reunification}}. A total of 1.6 million immigrants were admitted to Balisca in 2024, an increase from 700,000 in 2020. New immigrants settle mostly in major urban areas in the country, such as [[Castejón]], [[Concepción]] and [[Alessandria]]. Balisca also accepts large numbers of refugees, accounting for over x-percent of annual global refugee resettlements; it resettled more than 200,000 in 2024.

The first census in Balisca was carried out in 1800 and recorded a population of 5,236,631. From 1880 to 1930, 6 million immigrants arrived. Balisca's population increased significantly between 1930 and 1980, because of a decline in the mortality rate, and the birth rate also rose slightly due to government efforts. In the 1940s the annual population growth rate was 2.4%, rising to 3.0% in the 1950s and remaining at 2.9% in the 1960s, as life expectancy rose from 44 to 54 years and to 75.6 years in 2007. In 2024, the average {{W|total fertility rate}} (TFR) across Balisca was 1.71 children born per woman, on par with other countries in the Western world, but well below the replacement rate of 2.1. It remains considerably below the high of 5.11 children born per woman in 1865. In 2023, the median age in Balisca was 36.8 years old, above the world average (31.1), but younger than other developed countries.

The population of Balisca is expected to peak at 250 million people in 2050, and subsequently shrink to approximately 140 million people by 2150. Natural born Baliscans makeup 76% of the total population of Balisca. After the birth rate plunged in the 1970s and Balisca's population growth rate declined, the population again trended upward initially upon the return of many Baliscans who had emigrated to other Western countries during the 1970s, and more recently, fueled by large numbers of immigrants who make up x% of the population. In 2025, 72.8% of births were to [[Marriage in Balisca|unmarried parents]].

===Health and disability===
In common with many other {{W|developed countries}}, Balisca is experiencing an increase in healthcare expenditures due to a demographic shift toward an older population, with more retirees and fewer people of working age. A 2016 report by the chief public health official found that 92 percent of Baliscans, one of the highest proportions of the population among G8 countries, indicated that they "had good or very good health". Fifty percent of Baliscan adults self-report having at least one major risk factor for chronic disease: smoking, physical inactivity, unhealthy eating or excessive alcohol use. Balisca has one of the lowest rates of adult obesity among [[Abyalan Mutual Development Community]] (AMDC) countries, often attributed to high physical activity and the high prevalence of {{W|vegetarianism}} (accounting for 50–59% of all Baliscans). Three chronic diseases—cancer (leading cause of death), cardiovascular diseases and respiratory diseases—account for 85 percent of deaths in Balisca.

In 1998 the government estimated that there were 5,753,000 people with disabilities in Balisca, constituting about 4.8% of the total population. The totals of the three legally defined categories were: 3,170,000 {{W|physically disabled}}; 413,000 {{W|intellectually disabled}}; and 4,170,000 have [[W:mental disorder|psychiatric disabilities]]. The physically disabled category was made up of people with the following impairments: 1,657,000 (56.5%) mobility impairments; 305,000 (10.4%) are visually impaired; 350,000 (11.9%) are hard of hearing or deaf; while 621,000 (21.2%) had "internal disabilities" such as heart disease.

Discrimination against persons with disabilities in employment, education, access to health care, or the provision of other administrative services is illegal, and is rigorously targeted by regional and communal action through the [[Disabled Peoples' Fundamental Initiative]] (IFD). The law mandates access to public buildings and to newly built private buildings for such persons. {{W|Disability rights}} and policy have seen drastic reforms and advancements since the 1960s when the lack of rights of disabled people at the time was recognized as an issue by the general public. These rights are protected by both international law and domestic law. Balisca ratified the {{W|Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities}} (CRPD) in January 2007.

According to Article 1 of the IFD, Balisca defines an individual with a disability as one "whose daily life or life in society is substantially limited over the long term due to a physical, mental, or intellectual disability." In order to prevent discrimination and address the needs of those who qualify under this definition, Balisca has issued progressive measures for the social well being, inclusion and employment of people with disabilities.

===Ethnography===
{{Main|Ethnography of Balisca|Immigration to Balisca}}

[[File:Mapuche Machis.jpg|thumb|270x270px|[[W:Mapuche people|Wallche]] ''[[W:Machi (shaman)|machi]]'' (shaman of the [[Ítaca Islands|Könāgū nation]] of [[Tinguiririca]]) in 1913]]
Balisca does not collect data on ethnicities or "race" of its population. {{W|Anti-racism}} laws prohibit and penalize {{W|racial discrimination}} in housing, business, and health services. In 2022, the Baliscan Constitution was amended to officially renounce racial theory ("racialism") and [[w:Race (human categorization)|racial categorization]]. As a result, most demographic information is drawn from self-identification or academic institutions. According to the [[2025 Baliscan census]], over 450 "ethnic or cultural origins" were self-reported by [[Baliscans]]. The major {{W|Panethnicity|panethnic origin groups}} in Balisca are: [[European Baliscans|Illypnian]] (21%), [[Bayani Baliscans|Bayani]] (14.8%), [[East Fosian Baliscans|East Fosian]] (9.2%), [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|Indigenous]] (21.1%), [[Kaftia|Kaftian]] (24.8%), [[Ethnic groups in Abyala|Abyalan]] and [[Ethnic groups in Lurandia|Lurandian]] (12.5%),  [[Talokanil Sea|Talokanil]] (2.1%), Oceanian (0.6%), and Other (0.4%). [[Instituto Confederal de Stadística]] (ICS) reported that 64.8% of the population reported multiple ethnic origins, thus the overall total is greater than 100%. 

The country's ten largest self-reported specific ethnic or cultural origins in 2021 were Baliscan (accounting for 15.6 percent of the population), followed by [[W:Catalans|Volisan]] (14.7 percent), [[Moro people|Moros]] (12.1 percent), [[Araucaria|Araucarian]] (12.1 percent), [[W:Occitans|Beron]] (11.0 percent), [[Kurupiranga]] (4.9 percent), [[W:Mapuche people|Wallche]] (4.7 percent), [[W:Javanese people|Nusarayan]] (4.3 percent), [[W:Syrians|Lagashi]] (3.7 percent), and [[Imfezzan Baliscans|Imfezzan]] (3.5 percent). [[File:Andalusian Algarvean.png|left|thumb|300x300px|''Algarveño'' ''andaluces'' ("Andalusian [[Algarves|Algarvean]]") by [[Samara Al-Abbasi]] (1879)]]
A 2018 study conducted jointly by Balista and the Instituto Confederal de Stadística estimated that the largest ethnic groups were of [[West Kaftia|West]]/[[Central Kaftia|Central Kaftian]] origin (28 million), followed by [[South Kaftia|South Kaftian]] (17-18 million), [[W:Andalusians|Andalusian]] (12 million) [[W:Catalans|Volisanians]] (11.5 million), [[W:Occitans|Berons]] (9 million), [[W:Aragonese people|Xacano]] (4 million) Hersatian (3 million), and [[W:Basque people|Bizkarran]] (2 million). There are sizable minorities of other Illypnian ethnic groups, namely [[Ukrainian Baliscans|Kalinovans]], [[Iarmhaigh Baliscans|Iarmhaigh]], [[Sarmaci Baliscans|Sarmaci]], and [[Aetolians]]. Balisca has a significant {{W|Romani people|Romani}} (''Gitanos'') population, numbering between 200,000 and 400,000; many foreign ''Romani'' are formerly undocumented migrants that were facing expulsion from Illypnian countries who have been granted residency with a path to citizenship.

There is also a significant population of [[Baliscan Jews|Jewish Baliscans]], recorded at 1,607,387 people in 2025, or 1.04% of the population, measuring as the largest concentration of [[W:Jewish people|Jewish people]] in all of the Western Hemisphere. The vast majority of Jewish people in Balisca are of [[w:Sephardi|Sephardi]] origin, although there are smaller numbers of [[W:Sephardi Jews|Ashkenazi]] and [[w:Mizrahi Jews|Mizrahi Jews]].

Balisca is home to a significant population of [[The Levant|Levantine]] and [[Sudanese Kaftia|Sudanese]] descent, consisting of diverse [[W:Moriscos|Morisco]], [[Imfezzan Baliscans|Imfezzan]] and [[Moro people|Moro]] backgrounds. A plurality of [[Bayani Baliscans]] identify as [[w:Christians|Christians]] (as of the 2020 census), who belong to the {{W|Maronite Church}}, [[w:Roman Catholic Church|Petran Catholic]], [[w:Eastern Orthodox Churches|Eastern Orthodox]] and {{W|Eastern Rite Catholic Churches}}. The [[Fosian Baliscans|Fosian]] population in the country numbers at around 9.8 million individuals, most of whom are of [[North Fosia|North Fosian]] or [[Northeast Fosia|Northeast Fosian]] origin. The population of people of [[East Fosia]] descent has been slowly expanding since the beginning of the 21st century, mostly from [[Daras]].

A study conducted on 1,200 individuals in 2020 by the [[Instituto Confederal de Stadística]], has established that the genetic map of Balisca is composed by 19% from different Illypnian ethnicities (mainly Volisanian, Xacano, Beron and Parani ethnicities), 18% of different indigenous ethnicities, 8.8% from a variety of Fosian ethnicities, and 24.3% of Kaftian ethnic groups, in which 63.6% of the tested group had at least one ancestor who was [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|Indigenous]], most commonly of [[w:Caiçaras|Caiçara]] or [[Kurupiranga]] background.

Following the conclusion of the [[Baliscan War of Independence]], the fledgling confederation had a large territory but was thinly populated, and its ethnic composition was largely the same from the colonial era that had lasted from 16th to early 19th centuries. The first large-scale immigration began in the post-independence years with the arrival of Fosian (particularly [[Indian Baliscans|Kemali]], [[Sanese Baliscans|Sanese]] and [[Darasian Baliscans|Darasian]]) [[W:Employment contract|contract laborers]], which replaced slave labor following it's abolition in 1808. Following the [[Baliscan Civil War]], [[W:Free migration|constitutional policies that encouraged immigration]] were adopted, opening the national harbors to immigration from all continents, guaranteeing "free entry for persons healthy and able to work".  

From the 1970s, immigration has mostly been coming from [[Northern Fosia]] (particularly [[Kemal]], [[Mugladesh]] and [[Daras]]), alongside smaller numbers from [[Tamazgha]], [[Bonande]], [[Sindewand]] and [[Balausia]]. In 2019, the confederal government determined that around 2,750,000 residents lacked official residency documents, and subsequently implemented canton-level programs to encourage these undocumented individuals to declare their status in return for two-year residence visas. Since the implementation, over 800,000 applications have been processed. 

====Immigration and citizenship====
{{Main|Immigration to Balisca|Irregular immigration to Balisca}}

{{See also|Visa policy of Balisca}}
[[File:SS_Tartessia_arrival.png|thumb|330x330px|''[[Tricontinental Company|SS Tartessia]]'' nearing it's [[W:Berth (moorings)|berth]] in 1917, operating on the [[Tricontinental Company|Ampuria-Ishkal-Castejón route]]]]
[[File:La Baba immigration station.png|left|thumb|300x300px|[[La Baba Immigration Center]], now a preserved heritage site, welcomed millions of immigrants to the [[Alpujarra]] (c. 1914)]]
As with other areas of new settlement such as [[Cotochia]], [[Asikyira]], [[Almuria]], [[Kaskaskia]], the [[United Midlands]] and [[Balboa]], {{W|settler colonialism}} led to the displacement of indigenous peoples and mass immigration. Balisca is sometimes referred to as a ''mosaico cultural'' ({{W|cultural mosaic}}) of various ethnicities, ultimately developing a [[W:Polyethnicity|polyethnicity]]. 

Between 1857 and 1970, welcomed nearly 20 million immigrants, far ahead of other areas of new settlement. Strikingly, at those times, the national population doubled every two decades. This belief is endured in the popular saying ''"El baliscano yá vien outro lado del marín"'' ({{Literal translation|Baliscans came from across the sea}}).

Therefore, most Baliscans are descended from the 19th- and 20th-century immigrants of the [[Immigration to Balisca|great immigration wave to Balisca]] (1850–1955), with a slim majority of these immigrants coming from diverse countries around the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean Basin]]. The vast majority of these immigrants came from the [[Andalusian Peninsula]], [[Central Fosia]] and [[Western Kaftia]]. 

Out of all migrating populations in the colonial era, [[Andalusians]], [[Bayani Baliscans|Bayanis]], [[Volisanian Baliscans|Volisanians]], [[Beron Baliscans|Berons]], and [[Moro people|Moros]] were the most represented of those coming to the Baliscan territories. While the Volisanians attempted to discourage non-Mesogean immigration to its mainland colonies in an attempt to prevent other colonial powers countries from asserting claims, such prohibitions were rarely able to be enforced in the Baliscan colonies. 

Balisca today is considered the most popular immigration destination in the world, ahead of other countries such as [[Breisland]], [[Volisania]] and [[Burawa]]. It has the largest immigrant population in the world. Since decolonization, immigration has mostly been from countries like [[Tamazgha]], [[Dakare]], [[Daras]] and [[Balausia]], with smaller numbers from [[Bonande]], [[Lekkunto]], [[Aras]], and [[Sindewand]]. More recently, immigration from [[Northern Fosia]] (particularly [[Kemal]], [[Mugladesh]] and [[Daras]]) has increased substantially.{{Right|
