Since the end of the [[Great Adonian War]], individuals that have immigrated irregularly (to include {{W|refugees}}, {{W|asylum seekers}} and their families) have been able to seek rapid assistance by declaring their status to the [[Secretariat of International Affairs and Cooperation (Balisca)|Secretariat of International Affairs and Cooperation]] (SAIC). Local administrations have their own systems for [[social protection in Balisca|social protection]] programs, medical care, and education benefits for immigrants. SAIC offices overseas are also equipped to assist with {{W|LGBTQ migration}} from countries deemed hostile to {{W|LGBTQ rights}}. 
[[File:Nadie es ilegal banner.png.jpg|left|thumb|250x250px|''Nadie es ilegal'' ("[[W:No one is illegal|No one is illegal]]") banner in Castejón's [[Hisbanya]] borough]]
Since the progressive reforms of the during the [[Anyos Federales|Reconstruction era]], Balisca has maintained universal {{W|birthright citizenship}}, making every child born on Baliscan soil a [[Baliscan nationality law|Baliscan national]] with no regards to their parent’s origin. Additional reforms in the 1880s expanded the scope of this clause; children born to at least one Baliscan parent on foreign land was also considered a Baliscan national, allowing them all benefits of Baliscan citizenship. As a result of these relatively {{W|free migration}} policies, Balisca welcomed millions of immigrants that were subjected to racist {{W|nativism|nativist}} discrimination in [[Western world|Western countries]]. This has resulted in high levels of {{W|chain migration}} and the emergence of {{W|ethnic enclaves}}. Immigrants did not need a passport, visa, or any other document to enter the confederation. 

Transportation companies were in charge of all checks; if the entry was denied, the company was fined S/75 per each deported passenger, and covered the costs of their deportation. Initial immigration policy provided for the admission of most immigrants to Balisca, other than those with mental or physical disabilities.

The path to naturalization and Baliscan citizenship is open to all persons that have resided in Balisca continuously for a minimum of two years, passes an exam on "[[Baliscan values]]" and history, and expressed fluent knowledge of [[Baliscano]] or one of the territorialized languages.

=== Languages ===
{{Main|Languages of Balisca}}

[[File:Balisca languages map.svg|thumb|Linguistic map of Balisca, featuring territorialized languages, their dialects and indigenous languages (excludes Baliscano).]]
Balisca is a {{W|multilingualism|multilingual}} country, and multilingualism is widely encouraged at the communal and cantonal levels. [[Baliscano]]—the world's most widely spoken  [[W:Creole language|creole language]]—has effectively been the [[W:working language|working language]] of the entire confederation since 1883. As allowed in the third article of the Constitution, the other 'Baliscan languages' are recognized as official in their respective territories. The territoriality created by the form of co-officiality codified in the 1883 Constitution created an asymmetry, in which Baliscano was "confederally adopted" within the entire territory whereas vis-à-vis the rest of co-official languages, their status is recognized where it is spoken in their territories.

Besides Baliscano, other '''territorialized languages''' include the indigenous [[Kurupiran languages]], {{W|Aragonese language|Xacano}}, several of the {{W|Languages of Italy|Monsoran languages}} ("Monsoro"), [[W:Occitan language|Beron]], [[W:Basque language|Bizkarran]], {{W|Judaeo-Spanish language|Ladino}} (Judaeo-Sartan), [[W:Judaeo-Catalan|Judaeo-Volisan]] (Volisanit), [[Mozbayani language|Mozbayani]] and {{W|Tamazight}}, to which the {{W|Caló language|Caló}} and the sign languages may add up. The number of speakers varies widely and their legal recognition is uneven, with some of the most vulnerable languages lacking any sort of effective protection. 

As of 2022, Baliscano is estimated to be spoken proficiently by 84%, Volisanian by 30%, Mozbayani by 16% and both Kurupiran and Walldungu by around 4% of the Baliscan population. Some of the most spoken foreign languages used by the immigrant communities include [[W:Gulf Arabic|Parani]], [[W:Greek language|Aetolian]], {{W|Yoruba|Imo}} and [[W:Portuguese language|Kastrunetian]].

Due to demographic patterns and vast geographic diversity of Balisca, there are significant variations in {{W|dialect}}s amongst the cantons, although the preeminence of [[Castejón]] has led to wider adoption of the [[Castejón dialect|Alpujarran dialect]] of Baliscano, with all accented similarly to [[W:African Romance|Andalusian]] and [[W:Judaeo-Spanish|Ladino]]. Later waves of immigration brought [[W:Slang|slang]] and [[W:Cant (language)|cant]] such as [[W:Polari|Polari]] to Balisca. This informal speech influenced [[vesperiano|''vesperiano'']] (the [[Alessandria|Alessandrian]] dialect) which due to the former's status as the main gateway to Balisca, greatly influenced the development of Baliscano.

Through initiatives such as the [[Multilingualism Brings Opportunity Program]] which launched in 1923, the confederal administration implemented programs for students and the wider population as a whole to learn Baliscano alongside their {{W|first language}}, with languages classes for students in fifth-grade and above in public schools. These laws were amended in 1976, expanding its scope significantly, bringing it to every region across Balisca and introducing mandatory [[w:Language immersion|total immersion]] in all public learning institutions. Most cooperative schools in Balisca, for example, start teaching Baliscano from kindergarten, reflecting a trend that gained significant pace in the 1970s. Common Baliscano words have since been widely absorbed and appropriated into everyday speech.

There are several second-languages in widespread use among the Baliscan population, including:

