===Education===
{{Main|Education in Balisca}}

{{align|left|{{multiple image
| direction         = vertical
| align             = left
| width             = 250
| image1            = University of Poblenou.png
| caption1          = Founded in 1624, the [[University of Poblenou]] is one of Balisca's most prestigious, as well as one of the oldest in the country.
| image2            = UC Arcadia campus.png
| caption2          = [[La Universidá Cooperativa Arcadia]], a [[cooperative university]]. The residential college system consists of ten small colleges that were established as a {{W|Cooperative learning|collaborative}} variation of the {{W|collegiate university}} system.
}}
}}

The educational system is divided into {{W|preschool}} (for those under age 6), basic education (9 years, in three stages, compulsory), {{W|secondary education}} (3 years, compulsory since 1950), and higher education (subdivided in {{W|university}} and {{W|polytechnic}} education). Universities are usually organized into faculties. Institutes and schools are also common designations for autonomous subdivisions of Baliscan higher education institutions. Education in Balisca is based on {{W|cooperative learning}} and the {{W|Montessori education|Montessori method of education}}, which seeks to develop natural interests and activities rather than use formal teaching methods. During their nine years of common basic education, students are not selected, tracked, or streamed. There is also inclusive special education within the classroom and instructional efforts to minimize low achievement. There is no uniform curriculum across the Baliscan Confederation, with the {{W|libertarian municipalism|determination left}} to the individual [[Cantons of Balisca|cantons]], although some have chosen to cooperative at the [[regions of Balisca|regional level]]. 

Since the adoption of the [[Iniziatiba Basica Academic Confederau|Confederal Academic Baseline Initiative]] (IBAC) in 1922, the cantons authorized the establishment of confederal-level councils to compile statistics nationwide and make recommendations for baseline standards and requirements that each region would meet to. Regions that fail to meet the standards for a consecutive two schooling periods will have their programs abrogated by their region.

Prior to the adoption of IBAC, Balisca earlier pioneered universal, compulsory, and secular {{W|public education}} in 1852, after years of promotion at the confederal level by the Muslim and [[Catharism|Catharist]] communities.

The total adult literacy rate is 100 percent. Baliscan primary and secondary school enrollments are 100 percent and 100 percent respectively. According to the {{W|Programme for International Student Assessment}} (PISA) 2015, the average 15-year-old Baliscan student, when rated in terms of reading literacy, mathematics and science knowledge, is placed significantly above the OECD's average, at a similar level as those students from [[Skade]], [[Aetolia]], [[Kwangju]] and [[Okaiken]], with 501 points (493 is the average). The PISA results of the Baliscan student body have been continuously improving. 

Additionally, Balisca has one of the world's highest-educated labor forces among OECD countries. As of 2020, Balisca's public spending on education amounted to just 4.1 percent of its GDP, below the OECD average of 4.9 percent. Approximately 70 percent of Baliscans aged 25 to 34 have some form of tertiary education qualification, and bachelor's degrees are held by 38.2 percent of Baliscans aged 25 to 64.[[File:Baliscan Selectividad.jpg|thumb|250px|Students taking the [[Baliscan University Entrance Exam]], popularly known as the ''Selectividad'' during the {{W|COVID-19 pandemic}} in [[Merced]], [[Cerdanya]]]]About 58% of college-age citizens (20 years old) attend one of Balisca's higher education institutions (compared with 46% in [[Andalus]] and 35% in the other OECD countries). In addition to being a destination for international students, Balisca is also among the top places of origin for international students, a result of Balisca's well developed international ties. All higher education students, both domestic and international, totaled 10,680,937 in 2022. 
Baliscan universities have existed since the late 16th century. The oldest Baliscan university was first established in [[Bixessarri]] by Cathar settlers, before relocating to Cerdanya in 1686. 

Taking inspiration from several Illypnian countries, Balisca has adopted several reforms present in the {{W|Bologna process}} and has been widely implemented by Baliscan universities and poly-technical institutes. Admissions to universities are based on the high school final {{W|GPA}}, the high school final exam, and the university entrance examinations ([[Selectividad]]). The selection process for {{W|tertiary education}} is fully transparent, there are no application essays, no human factor in selection, support for underrepresented or disadvantaged minorities, and no weight on extracurricular activities. Moreover, the entrance examinations are rarely long multiple-choice exams, and instead consist of a smaller number of longer and more complicated questions that are supposed to test more than memorization and quick mechanical problem solving.

===Religion===
{{Main|Religion in Balisca|Irreligion in Balisca}}

{{See also|Christianity in Balisca|Islam in Balisca}}{{Pie chart|thumb=right|caption=Religion in the Baliscan Confederation (2025)|label2=[[Petran Catholic Church|Petran Catholic]]|value2=10.0|color2=Honeydew|label3=[[Catharism|Cathar]]|value3=9.8|color3=DarkOrchid|label1={{W|Irreligious}}|value1=50.2|color1=DodgerBlue|label4={{W|African diaspora religions|Kaftian Diasporic}}|value4=6.3|color4=Turquoise|label5=Other Christian churches (incl. [[w:Protestantism|Protestant]], [[w:Eastern Orthodox Church|Eastern]] and [[w:Oriental Orthodox Churches|Oriental Orthodox]])|value5=7.0|color5=Chartreuse|label6=[[wikipedia:Islam|Islam]]|value6=11.2|color6=DarkGReen|label7=[[w:Hinduism|Hinduism]]|value7=1.5|color7=Orange|label8=[[w:Sikhism|Sikhism]]|value8=1.4|color8=DarkKhaki|label9=[[w:Buddhism|Buddhism]]|value9=1.1|color9=Yellow|label10=[[w:Jewish people|Jewish]]|value10=1.0|color10=Hotpink|label11=Others/undeclared|value11=0.5|color11=Black}}[[Basic Law: Freedom of Religious Belief|Article Three of the Baliscan Basic Laws]] states: 

{{quote|''La libertad de relixón, de conciencia y la libertas de declarar les sos creencies relixoses o filosófiques son inviolables. Garantízase la práctica relixosa privada ensin intromisiones.''<br/>

"The freedom of religion, conscience and the freedom of confessing one's religious or philosophical beliefs are inviolable. Uninfringed private religious practice is guaranteed"}}

In Balisca, "freedom of religious belief" is used as a more broad replacement for both "freedom of religion" and "freedom from religion". The term "belief" is considered inclusive of all forms of {{W|irreligion}}, including {{W|atheism}}, {{W|antitheism}}, [[W:Antireligion|antireligion]] and {{W|humanism}}. The practice of religion has long been legally and culturally considered a strictly private matter throughout society and government. 

In addition, the right to actively practice one's religion without fear of repercussion is one of the original eight "fundamental freedoms" guaranteed by the original Baliscan constitution. Baliscan history has been marked with religious pluralism and tolerance, first as a result of [[W:Religious persecution|religious persecution]] prompted by militant Catholicism in the [[Marañón basin]], and later demographic shifts from diverse immigration from throughout the world. There has never been a single dominating religion at any point in post-colonial Baliscan history. The vast majority of Baliscans consider [[w:Importance of religion by country|religion to be unimportant]] in their daily lives, with a very small minority (~3%) still believing in a deity or a spiritual entity.  

Following the civil war, laws were adopted to prevent the establishment of a {{W|state religion}} at all levels of government, and prevented the government from involving itself in the private religious affairs of Baliscan citizens. Additionally, It allowed the right to believe what a person, group, or {{W|religion}} wishes, but it does not recognize the right to practice the religion or belief openly and outwardly in a public manner, which is central facet of {{W|religious freedom}}. 

Since the implementation of {{W|secularism}}, the Baliscan government went on to adopt the principle of ''{{W|laïcité|laïcitat}}'', in which the state does not recognize any official ''religion''. Instead, it merely recognizes certain religious organizations, according to formal legal criteria (adopted via consensus) that do not address religious doctrine. In return, religious organizations are to refrain from involvement in policy-making or any activity that could be interpreted as political in a court of law.
[[File:Misión de San Agustín de Xaraba (expanded).png|left|thumb|320x320px|The [[Misión de San Agustín de Xaraba]] is the largest church in Balisca]]
Islam was introduced in the [[Northern Triangle]] following the arrival of explorers and colonists on behalf of the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania|Crown of Volisania]] the late 15th to early 16th century, after which many were forced to choose between conversion or expulsion to the colony. Significantly larger numbers of enslaved Kaftian Muslims were brought to the Baliscan colonies following the beginning of the [[Ethiopian slave trade]]. Historically, an estimated 60% of slaves brought to Balisca from [[Kaftia|Kaftians]] were Muslims. They were overwhelmingly literate in contrast to many within the slave owning class. [[W:Syncretism|Syncretism]] between traditional Kaftian religions, Christianity, and Islam from the 16th century forward eventually gave rise to syncretic religions like [[Lucumí Tarica]] (which centers upon the concepts of [[W:Orisha|Orisha]] in [[W:Yoruba religion|Sanyan religion]] and the [[W:Jinn|Jinn]] of [[W:Islamic mythology|Islamic mythology]]), and are considered sister religions with [[W:Santería|Santería]] and [[Asikyira|Asikyiran]] [[W:Candomblé|Candomblé]].[[File:Gran Mesquita de l'Alhambra.jpg|thumb|280x280px|The [[Great Mosque of Alhambra|Gran Miskita di Alħambra]] in [[Cabu Verd]] is one of the most famous [[W:Mosque|mosques]] in Balisca.]]
Following the outbreak of the [[Maroon Wars]], a significant number of enslaved people rebelled across the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|Western Plains]], leading to a violent campaign of self-liberation across the agricultural heartland. Thousands escaped, some with the help of the [[Catharism|Catharist]] community which was vehemently opposed to slavery. This resistance coalesced into the [[Araucarian Passage]]. Many freed persons later became involved in the movement of those liberated through the Passage, often with the cooperation of the Cathar community in their activity, and often proclaimed membership while in colonial territory to reduce suspicion of their activity. Additionally, the rise of the [[Maroon pirates]] and the founding of [[Daramazán]], heralded extensive exchange and cooperation between formerly enslaved and the largely Muslim clans.  

According to the Center for Sociological Research, 10.0% of citizens self-identify as {{W|Catholics}}, (8.7% define themselves as not practicing, while 2.3% as practicing), 9.8% as [[Catharism|Cathars]], 7% self-identified with another Christian denomination, 22.5% as followers of other faiths (including {{W|Islam}}, {{W|Hinduism}}, {{W|Traditional African religions|Traditional Kaftian religions}}, [[W:Judaism|Judaism]], etc.), and 50.2% identify as {{W|atheists}} (21.9%), [[W:Agnostics|agnostics]] (20.6%) or non-believers (7.7%) as of April 2025. 

According to the same poll, the proportion of the unaffiliated is higher among the youth, about 79% among 18-21 year olds. An overwhelming majority of Baliscans do not participate regularly in weekly religious worship. A July 2021 study shows that of the Baliscan who identify themselves as religious, 66% never attend services, 20.8% barely ever attend services, 19% attend a few times a year, 6.8% two or three times per month, 13.4% every holidays, and 0.3% multiple times per week.

==Geography==
{{Main|Geography of Balisca|Baliscan archipelago}}

[[File:Sonoro Balisca.png|thumb|250x250px|[[Tanimbuka]] is the highest mountain in the [[Northern Hemisphere|Northern]] and [[Western Hemisphere|Western Hemispheres]] at {{Convert|5,222.6|m|ft|abbr=on}}]]
[[File:Balisca relief map.png|left|thumb|307x307px|Relief map of Balisca]]
Balisca occupies roughly forty percent of the land area of [[Abyala]] (when including the [[Haitan]] subcontinent), covering a large area along its northern extremity (known as the [[Northern Triangle]]) and much of the continent's interior. Its only land borders are with [[Asikyira]] and [[Potocsí]] to the south. Balisca has a total land area of {{convert|4688451.45|km2}}, making it the [[List of countries and dependencies by area|third-largest country by area in the world]]. Because of its far-flung outlying islands and long coastline, the country has extensive marine resources. Its [[w:Exclusive economic zone of Balisca|exclusive economic zone]] is one of the largest in the world, covering more than 3 times its land area. The country, including all of the islands it controls, lies between the {{W|Tropic of Capricorn}} and the [[Arctiki Circle]].

The Baliscan mainland is divided along its length by the [[Matarrañya|Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]], separating the eastern quarter of the country from the western three-quarters. There are 18 peaks over {{convert|3000|m|ft}}, the highest of which is is [[Tanimbuka]] (‘ash mountain’) in the Matarrañya / Cañadapirén, about {{Convert|5,222.6|m|ft|abbr=on}} metres above sea level. About 25 million years ago, a shift in {{W|plate tectonic}} movements began to [[w:Kaikoura orogeny|contort and crumple]] the region. This is now most evident in the Matarrañya / Cañadapirén, formed by [[w:Continental collision|compression of the crust]] beside the [[? Fault]].

Among the outstanding geographical elements are:

* The [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] mountain range, which in the [[Northern Triangle]] reaches the highest elevations in the [[Western Hemisphere]] and the [[Northern Hemisphere]] (more specifically: the highest elevations on the planet outside [[Fosia]]); by contrast, also in the Northern Triangle are the largest continental depressions in the boreal hemisphere and in all of the Algarves below sea level ([[Gran Baix de les Hespèrides]]).
* The extensive and fertile plains of the [[Caguasu|Kaá-Guasu]] ({{Literal translation|great grassland}}) contrasted by the so-called [[Arid Diagonal of Balisca|arid diagonal]] that extends from the [[Tinguirirican Desert|Tinguiririca Desert]], the most [[Aridity|arid]] in the Algarves, to certain semi-desert regions of the coast of [[Western Tinguiririca]].
* The [[Tinguirirican Plateau]], especially the [[Archipelago of Patagonia|Patagonian archipelago]] and its continuation on the marine platform, the [[Illes de Sant Pere]].
* The large estuaries and rivers: the largest [[W:River|river]]/[[W:Estuary|estuary]] by discharge on Adonia (the [[Marañón River|Marañón]]), the great rivers of the [[Marañón basin]] such as the [[Paraná River|Paraná]], [[Aín (river)|Aín]], [[Canyada River|Canyada]], [[Tapajós River|Tapajós]] (with the [[Tapajós Falls|falls]] of the same name), [[Daimús River|Daimús]], [[Bermejo River|Bermejo]], [[Salado River (northern Argentina)|Salado del Norte]] which form a feedback system with the gigantic [[Guaraní Aquifer]] which emerges in vast [[W:Wetland|wetlands]] such as the [[Pantanal|Great Pantanal]] or the [[Quiscosta Wetlands]].
* The [[Salsipuedes Sea]], one of the most extensive [[W:Continental shelf|epicontinental shelves]], and for this reason submarine, on Adonia.
* The [[Tinguirirican Ice Field|Tinguiririca ice fields]] accompanied by large active [[W:Glacier|glaciers]].
* Large and important lacustrine systems of glacial origin (produced after the retreat of the last [[Last Glacial Period|Würmian]] glaciation some 11,000 years ago) such as those found in northern Cerdanya and in the Matarrañya / Cañadapirén region of [[Araucaria]] and [[Hesperia]].
* The southern part of the massif commonly called [[Maranyon Massif|Maranyon]].

The [[geology]] of Balisca is complex: all the emerged territory corresponds to the [[South American Plate|Abyalan Plate]], which is quite stable in most of the territory, which means that most of the [[Northern Triangle]] is exempt from important earthquakes. However, the northern and eastern edges of this plate are in permanent friction and collision with the [[Hesperian Plate]] which drifts westward; the western extreme of the [[Northern Triangle]] forms part of the [[Ring of Fire]] which north of [[Cap Jaumandreu]] continues through the [[Aurora Islands]] and the [[Ártico-Matarrañya|Ártica-Matarrañya]]. The collision of both plates has provoked the main recent orographic foldings (the range is very slowly continuing to rise), and this friction on the east coast continues to release enormous amounts of energy by producing [[W:Earthquake|earthquakes]] of subduction in the form of megathrust earthquakes that mainly affect central and south-central Balisca and the [[Castejón Metropolis]]. It should be noted that the largest earthquake recorded by seismographs occurred in the [[Alpujarra]] in the year [[1924 Alpujarra earthquake|1924]].

From east to west after the [[W:Andean folding|Matarrañyan folding]] with its many volcanoes (the vast majority of them inactive), there are [[W:Graben|tectonic trenches]] and ''[[W:Horst (geology)|horsts]]'', while the glacial lakes are dammed by hills of [[W:Moraine|morainic]] origin (''[[W:Drumlin|drumlins]]'') that partially form the orographic system of the [[Patagonides]], the drumlins of   Tinguiririca, or channel the water flows produced by the heavy precipitation in the Matarrañya / Cañadapirén mountain range toward the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] creating rivers of very short length although very voluminous ([[Alagón river|Alagón]], [[tba]], [[tba]], [[tba]], [[tba]], etc.) or dam the waters that are tributaries of the Atlantic Ocean creating extensive lakes of glacial origin (such as [[Llanquihue]], [[tba]], [[tba]], [[tba]], [[tba]], [[tba]], [[tba]], [[tba]], etc.). The retreat of the great glaciers of the [[Last Glacial Period|Würmian]] has left isolated mountains between stepped plateaus, [[W:Basalt|basaltic]] and [[W:Granite|granitic]] mountains called chihuidos very close to extensive canyons and [[W:Ría|rías]] in [[Tinguiririca|Northern Tinguiririca]] and [[W:Fjord|fjords]] in the extreme northwest of [[Cap de Pingüins]] (even including [[Illes de Sant Pere]]). On the other hand, the maritime straits such as the [[tba]] and the [[tba]] are ancient valleys carved by glaciers, which at the end of the Würmian glaciation and upon the withdrawal of the enormous glaciers, were inundated by marine waters.

North of the Bayaran [[Río Negro (Bayara)|Río Negro]], a river that largely flows along the geological fault line that separates the [[Maranyon Massif|Maranyon]] [[Maranyon Massif|massif]] from the [[Tinguiririca massif]], the lands are much flatter and extend all the way to the [[Salsipuedes Sea]]. It includes the very flat and fertile extensive [[tba plain]], largely covered by a type of [[W:Podzol|podzol]] soil and especially of [[W:Loess|loessic]] type, which is rarely interrupted in its current relief by low-altitude mountain systems, very eroded remnants of ancient mountain ranges.<gallery widths="200" perrow="3" mode="packed" caption="The varied landscapes of Balisca">
