=== Climate ===
{{Main|Climate of Balisca}}

[[File:Central Balisca coastal fog.jpg|thumb|320x320px|[[W:Upswelling|Upswelling]] of [[Aurora Current|cold sub-surface water]] creates the offshore fog that is characteristic of the [[Levante, Balisca|Levante]]]]
The climate of Balisca is a complex subject: the vast size of the country and wide variation in altitude make for a wide range of climate types. The diverse climate of Balisca ranges from one of the world's driest deserts in the south—the [[Akûtukû Desert]]—through a [[W:Mediterranean climate|Mesogean climate]] in the center, [[W:Humid subtropical climate|humid subtropical]] in the [[Marañón basin]] and the coast of the [[Salsipuedes Sea]], to an [[W:Oceanic climate|oceanic climate]], including [[W:Alpine tundra|alpine tundra]] and glaciers in the north and northeast. According to the Köppen system, Balisca within its borders hosts at least eighteen major climatic subtypes. The dominant general climatic types of Balisca are temperate; cool temperatures and frequent cloudy skies are common for most of the country east of the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]]. Under the [[W:Köppen climate classification|Köppen climate classification]], a [[W:Warm-summer Mediterranean|warm-summer Mediterranean]] (''Csb'') designation is assigned to many areas of the [[Alpujarra]] as far north as [[Syracuse]] and as far south as the [[Axarquía]] (coastal area), including cities such as [[Castejón]], [[Yerba Buena]], [[Jatiel]], and [[Amposta]]. This is due to the cool offshore, enhanced by [[W:Upwelling|upwelling]] of cold sub-surface waters, which results in a summer fog near the coast.
[[File:Pan de Azucar National Park.jpg|left|thumb|300x300px|[[Pan de Asúkar National Park]] in the southern extremity of the [[Akûtukû Desert]] ([[Tukupi|Tukûybi]]).]]
An [[W:Alpine climate|Alpine climate]] dominates in the high mountains, especially so in [[Cerdanya]]. [[W:Semi-arid|Semi-arid]] and [[W:Arid|arid]] climates are found west of the higher mountains, especially in [[W:Rainshadow|rainshadow]] areas. The [[Tinguiririca Desert]] within the [[Arid diagonal of Balisca|arid diagonal]] is an example of arid climate in [[Abyala]], and is considered the driest location in the world. [[W:Humid continental|Humid continental]] climates occur inland on windward sides, in places such as [[Merced]] and [[Concepción]] due to the cold [[Aurora Current]]. A [[W:Oceanic climate#Subpolar oceanic and cold subtropical highland varieties (Cfc, Cwc)|subpolar climate]] can be found farther west, especially in [[Cap de Pingüins]] and it's far flung insular territories.

Because much of the country is moderated by the surrounding oceans, winters are often less intense and prolonged than areas at similar latitudes in the [[Northern Hemisphere]]. Spring and autumn are transition seasons that generally feature mild weather. The average annual precipitation ranges from 150 millimetres (6 in) in the driest parts of the Ítaca Islands to over 2,000 millimetres (79 in) in the southernmost parts of [[Pindorama]] and the northwestern parts of the [[Northern Triangle]]. [[Castejón]], [[Jatiel]] and [[Carabassa]] all receive a yearly average of more than 2,000 hours of sunshine. The northwestern parts of Cerdanya have colder and cloudier climates, with around 1,400–1,600 hours; the central region of the island is the sunniest area of the country and receives about 2,400–2,500 hours. 

Many regions have different, often contrasting, microclimates. In general, western and southern parts of the country (such as the [[Marañón basin]]) are characterized by hot, humid, rainy summers and mild winters with periodic [[W:Drought|droughts]]. Pindorama is characterized by high temperatures and abundant precipitation throughout the year with droughts being uncommon. South of this lies the [[Mesopotàmia]] region, which is the hottest region in Balisca. Precipitation in Mesopotàmia decreases eastwards, resulting in the vegetation changing from {{W|forests}} in the east to shrubs in the west. Northern Balisca, especially in the lowlands is predominantly wet and cool, although the rugged topography makes it climatically diverse, ranging from the hot, wet [[Guarapiranga]], to the dense [[Hesperian Aethiopic Forest]].

Balisca is vulnerable to and will likely be significantly impacted by {{W|climate change}}. Temperatures have increased in the last century while the observed changes in precipitation are variable, with some areas receiving more and other areas less. These changes have impacted river flow, increased the frequency of [[W:Extreme weather events|extreme weather events]], and led to the [[W:Glacier retreat|retreat of glaciers]]. Based on the projections for both precipitation and temperatures, these climatic events are likely to increase in severity and create new problems associated with climate change in the country.
====Biodiversity====
The [[wildlife of Balisca]] comprises all naturally occurring animals, plants, and fungi in the Abyalan country. Home to 70% of the [[Marañón rainforest]], which accounts for approximately one-tenth of all species in the world, Balisca is considered to have the greatest [[W:Biodiversity|biodiversity]] of any country on the planet, containing over 70% of all animal and plant species catalogued. Balisca has the most known species of plants (55,000), freshwater fish (3,000) and mammals (over 689). It also ranks third on the list of countries with the most bird species (1,832) and second with the most reptile species (744). The number of fungal species is unknown but is large. Since the arrival of humans around 13,000 BCE, almost eight percent of the Balisca's {{W|vertebrate}} species have become extinct, including at least thirty-three birds, three frogs, three lizards, one freshwater fish, and one bat. Others are endangered or have had their range severely curtailed. However, Balisca's [[Ecological Action|active environmentalists and conservationists]] have pioneered numerous methods to help threatened wildlife recover, including island sanctuaries, pest control, wildlife translocation, {{W|facilitating rewilding}} and {{W|ecological restoration}} of islands, {{W|wetlands}} and other protected areas.  

Today, nearly seventy-five percent of Balisca is forested, mountainous or unsuitable for agricultural, industrial or residential use. As a result, the habitable zones, mainly located in [[Megaregions of Balisca|coastal areas]], have unusually high population densities, often resulting in a very dense [[W:Wildland–urban interface|wildland–urban interface]] with higher risk of destructive wildfires.  [[File:Sequoia sempervirens Ybyrá-arama.png|thumb|372x372px|Coastal redwoods ([[W:Sequoia sempervirens|''Sequoia sempervirens'']]) in [[Añãngaraçú]]'s [[Ybyrá-arama Ecological Reserve]].]]
{{multiple image
