Before the arrival of humans, an estimated 85% of the land was {{W|forest cover|covered}} in {{W|forest}}, with only {{W|tree line|high alpine}}, wet, infertile and volcanic areas without trees. Massive deforestation occurred after humans arrived, with around 10% the forest cover lost to fire after [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|indigenous]] settlement. A significant portion of the remaining forest fell after Illypnian settlement, being logged or cleared to make room for pastoral farming. The process accelerated further with the arrival of the {{W|Industrial Revolution}} and {{W|Urban sprawl|rapid urbanization}}, which provoked political and military conflicts with [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|Indigenous tribes]]. The [[1883 Confederal Convention]] resulted in the restoration of [[W:Indigenous sovereignty|indigenous sovereignty]], and successive governments since then have sought to conserve Balisca's forest through indigenous protection. As a result of {{W|reforestation}} projects, forest cover has climbed to 68% of the land in 2025. Balisca along with [[Asikyira]] are the first countries to have halted [[W:Deforestation|commercial/industrial deforestation]]; it has developed an ecosystem service to teach biologists and ecologists about its environmental protection measures.[[File:Harpyhaliaetus coronatus -captive-8b.jpg|thumb|345x345px|The [[W:Chaco eagle|crowned solitary eagle]] (''Buteogallus coronatus'') is distributed throughout western Atlantida and Pindorama.]]The native flora of Balisca consists of similar species to that found in other Abyalan countries. In western Balisca, heavy precipitation has produced dense forests of Balisca's iconic {{W|Redwoods}}, {{W|Douglas fir|Baliscan pine}}, and various species of {{W|conifers}} and {{W|beeches}}, which become smaller and more stunted as one moves further north. The high coastal mountains of the east coast trap moisture from the clouds: fog in summer, rain, and snow in winter creating a favorable environment for the [[W:Coastal redwood|coastal redwood]] (''Sequoia sempervirens'') trees. 

The cold temperatures and winds of the extreme north preclude heavy forestation. Grassland is found in [[Willi Mapu]] and [[Máfil]]. Some of northern Balisca’s flora has an Arktic origin due to land bridges which formed during the [[W:Cretaceous|Cretaceous]] ice ages, allowing plants to migrate from [[Arktici]] to the [[Tinguirirican Peninsula]]. The same holds for the southern [[Marañón basin]] in relation to the territory of [[Pindorama]], which has a level of [[W:Megadiverse countries|megadiversity]] that is dominated by [[W:Tropical and subtropical moist broadleaf forests|tropical and subtropical moist broadleaf forests]]. This has given rise to vibrant faunal diversity, in which many distinctive Abyalan animals are found. Among the mammals are capybara, [[W:Jaguars|jaguars]], [[W:jaguarundi|jaguarundi]], [[W:White-eared opossum|white-eared opossum]], the [[W:maned wolf|maned wolf]] and the [[W:Marsh deer|marsh deer]]. In the northern regions, several types of marsupials, deer (the [[W:Pudu|pudu]]), and the [[W:Guanaco|guanaco]] are found. In the far southeast, other camelids such as [[W:Llamas|llamas]] and [[W:Alpacas|alpacas]] are common.

The northeastern extremities of the Marañón basin (roughly correlating to [[tba]] in the [[Bayara|Bayaran]] community, is dominated by [[W:Mediterranean forests, woodlands, and scrub|Mesogean forests, woodlands, and scrub]], owing to the influence of the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] which blocks cool precipitation from the North Ethiopic and forms a [[W:Rain shadow|rain shadow]]. 

There are many species of small birds, but most of the larger common Abyalan types are absent. Few {{W|freshwater fish are native}}, but efforts in the late 1900s resulted in the successfully introduction of Lurandian and Illypnian freshwater species into the Bayaran and Cerdanyan lake systems. Owing to the the South-flowing [[Baliscan Current]], ocean waters abound with fish and other forms of marine life, which in turn support a rich variety of {{W|waterfowl}}, including several {{W|penguins}}. {{W|Whales}} are abundant throughout the waters surrounding Balisca, and some six species of {{W|seals}} are found in the area.

===Environmental issues===
====Climate change====
{{See also|Green Protocol}}[[File:Balisca chinampa.jpg|thumb|A large {{W|Community-supported agriculture|agricultural cooperative}} utilizing the {{W|chinampa}} technique in [[Aitona]] ([[Estuaria]]). This Indigenous practice is threatened by increased water consumption, flooding, and rising temperatures.]]
In regards to {{W|climate change}}, {{W|glacier depletion}}, {{W|sea-level rise}} and the increase in occurrence and intensity of [[North Ethiopic tropical cyclone]] (called ''sulcarano'' in Baliscano; a blend of ''sulcar'' “to plow” or “to cross” and the ''uragano'' “storm”) are the largest concerns for Balisca. The country is expected to get hotter and wetter with climate change, with some parts of the country becoming more tropical, with more extreme rain events.  
[[File:Bapo satellite photo.png|left|thumb|375x375px|[[North Ethiopic tropical cyclone]] Bapo, making land fall near [[Orquívoro]] on 5 February 2015]]
The [[Bayara|Bayaran]] and [[Hesperia]]n coast are the most likely to be affected by these changes. Additionally, the [[Marañón Delta]], home to some thirty million Baliscans, are faced with the possibility of their {{W|inundation}} and {{W|salinization}} by the [[Mar de Colores]] in the near future. In 2019, the Baliscan government revealed that the wetlands were disappearing at a rate of 8% per year, higher than the previous 2% declared in 2015.  Meanwhile, Hesperia, constituting roughly half of Balisca’s exposed Ethiopic coastline has been forced to prepare for the increasing occurrence of stronger hurricanes in the [[Northern Hemisphere]], along with increasing landslides from increased rainfall.  

In 2022, a survey of 10,000 people across Balisca, found that 96% of Baliscans agreed that climate change was a significant problem and was important for the country to address. 

Balisca was first struck by a major tropical cyclone in 1983, when the Category 4 {{W|subtropical cyclone}} [[Sulcarano Amara|''Amara'']] ("bitter") battered the [[Concordia|Concordia conurbation]] before moving north and dissipating over the island of [[Sicania]]. Heavy rainfall and record storm surge triggered massive {{W|landslide}}s all along the coast. In the chaos, over 3.3 million people were without power for four days, while several sections of the [[Levantine Link (Balisca)|Levantine Link]] were blocked, preventing the rapid dispersal of emergency services from the north. Low-lying areas around [[Castejon]] were completely submerged, stranding another two million people. Once the storm had passed, over 320 people had died and ε15.4 billion in damages had been caused. 

Since the disaster in 1983, Balisca has invested substantial resources in combating climate change and flooding. Taking inspiration from the Netherlands and the {{W|MOSE Project}} in {{W|Venice}}, Balisca is embarking on a large climate-change adaptation system. Since 1983, Balisca has been struck by storms of varying intensity every-year, with the most notable in 2004, 2010, 2015, 2016, 2017, 2019 and {{W|Subtropical Storm Yakecan|2022}}. A major milestone was reached in 2017, with the completion of the [[Augusta Storm Surge Barrier]], shielding the [[Augustinian Sea]] and its coasts from storm surge and rising sea levels.

Shortly after the discovery of the {{W|Ozone depletion|hole in the ozone layer}} above [[Arktici]] in 1982, Balisca became the first countries to declare a [[w:Climate emergency declaration|climate emergency]]. The administration passed legislation committing the Baliscan government to introduce measures to reduce CO2 emission below their 1990 levels, facilitate the development of {{W|green technology}}, and eliminate dependence on {{W|fossil fuels}}. Subsequent administrations embraced the adoption of new technology in combination with Indigenous land management and farming practises, such as {{W|Chinampa|chinampa}}, {{W|terrace farming}} and {{W|controlled burn|controlled forest burning}}. Balisca fully {{W|fossil fuel divestment|divested from fossil fuels}} in 2015, becoming the first country to do so.

In response to the economic slowdown caused by the [[w:COVID-19 pandemic|COVID-19 pandemic]], the Baliscan government passed several initiatives aimed at encouraging [[w:Green recovery|green recovery]] with a large [[W:Stimulus package|stimulus package]]. In total, Balisca's [[Green Protocol]] was allocated an unprecedented ε207 billion towards sustainable development (including renewable energy sources, eco-communities, and research into new environmental technology), and another pledged ε1.1 trillion in funding through 2050.

====Population density and urban expansion====
