Rapidly increasing density in urban areas has become a major issue for the urban governments as they grapple with militant opposition to urban expansion from rural communities led by the [[Ecological Action]]. Management of {{W|urban sanitation}} and transportation systems have become a considerably large headache for local governments. These issues are further complicated by Balisca’s geographic limitations: over 75% of the country is covered by forests and mountains, 40% of the country’s territory consists of {{W|protected land}}, and significant swathes of the country are {{W|seismically active}}. As a result, the 25% of land that is built up for settlement (about {{convert|267,478.75|km2}} has substantially higher population densities estimated at 542.16 people/sq km (209.33 people/sq mi) in 2022. Three-fourths of Balisca's population in 2022 lived on just 20% of the land. This ever-increasing density has prompted an increase in demand for [[Planned communities in Balisca|planned communities]] ,which has in turn triggered aggressive encroachment against rural areas. However this has worsened the ongoing land shortage crisis in Baliscan suburbs and led to rapid {{W|Brusselization|Kaspization}} and the transformation of sleepy communities into {{W|satellite city|satellite cities}}.

The proportion of Baliscans living in urban areas increased between 1950 and 2000, climbing from 31% to 90% while in the same period the rate of urban residential construction climbed 250%. Since the [[October 76|events of October 1976]], numerous Baliscan cities have begun building massive {{W|public housing estate|communal}} or {{W|housing cooperative|cooperatively run}} {{W|housing estate}}s, marketed as ''multo altos'' (meaning "many high" or "very deep"). These have found large success, stemming from influxes of immigrants seeking affordable housing and the rise of the ''[[Desconexión]]'' ("unplug" or "disconnection") sub-culture which has embraced communal living.

Several cities are unable to [[W:Municipal annexation|expand their borders]] (geographic constraints or communal opposition), and largely look upwards to solve the issue of lack of available land. Castejón, the confederal capital, is surrounded on all sides by {{W|satellite cities}} within other [[republics of Balisca|communities]], which has permanently prevented any further [[W:Municipal annexation|outward expansion]] of the Metropolis. [[Concepción]], the largest city in [[Cerdanya]], has taken a unique approach in expanding downwards, with [[Underground Concepción]]. This concept was later adopted into Castejón, with the {{W|mixed-use development|mixed-use}} [[Castejón Lowline]]. 

[[Estuaria]] is geographically very low relative to {{W|sea level}} and is considered a flat country, with about 26% of its area and 31% of its population located below sea level, and only about 50% of its land exceeding one metre above sea level. The mainland part of the country is for the most part flat, with the exception of foothills in the southeast, up to a height of no more than 121 metres, and some low hill ranges in the central parts. Most of the areas below sea level are caused by peat extraction or achieved through land reclamation. Since the late 16th century, large [[W:Polder|polder]] areas are preserved through elaborate drainage systems that include {{W|levee|dikes}}, canals and pumping stations. Nearly 17% of the community's land area is reclaimed from the sea (such as [[Navassa]]) and from the [[Garrigàs Gulf]] ([[Algesires]] with the [[Mar Menor Works]]).
[[File:Maracaju verticalization.png|left|thumb|Development of [[W:Tower block|tower block]] [[W:Housing estate|housing estates]] in [[Maracaju]] ({{Circa|2025}})]]
Several reports have shown that with projected population trends, Balisca’s population could surpass 240 million people by 2050, reflecting an increase of 30 million people in 30 years. Successive Baliscan governments have estimated that the country will have to add at least half a million new housing units per year in order to keep up with population growth and migration. The population is estimated to increase by around 800,000 people per year, with an additional estimated 750,000 to 1.2 million immigrants entering the country each year.

These issues were compounded by the outbreak of the {{W|COVID-19 pandemic}} in Balisca, which led to an exodus of residents from densely populated cities into smaller, less populous communities that are more spacious and more closely connected to nature. The subsequent reopening of the Baliscan economy, combined with the prevalence of underutilized commercial property and a surge in residential demand led to sharp increases in housing costs and a critical shortage of suitable units ([[Baliscan housing shortage#Housing insecurity|mostly affecting non-citizen communities]]). The shortage of [[W:Private housing|private housing]] to meet the soaring demand has intensified the ongoing [[Baliscan housing crisis]]. The confederal government of [[Zavier Bilal]] has responded with the introduction of the [[Casajuan Plan|Urban Redevelopment Project]] (popularly known as the "Casajuan Plan", after [[Raïmon Casajuan|its developer]]), in which communal governments are tasked with identifying unused or underutilized retail and office space that can be re-developed into residential space.

=== Wildfires ===
[[File:Trás-os-Montes wildfire.png|thumb|A firefighting volunteer monitoring the approach of flames during the [[2021 Trás-as-Montanyas wildfires]].]]
[[W:Wildfire|Wildfires]] in Balisca are occurring more frequently and are growing more dangerous because of the accumulation of wood fuel in forests, increasingly densely populated [[W:Wildland-urban interface|wildland-urban interface]] (WUI) areas, [[W:global warming|global warming]] attributed to [[W:Climate change|climate change]], and greater electricity transmission and distribution lines. Baliscans contribute about ε13 billion a year to combat wildfires (to include both controlled and uncontrolled methods), and big fires can lead to billions of dollars in property losses. Beginning in the 2020s, these wildfires have increasingly been fanned or made worse by strong and dry winds when they occur on Balisca's eastern seaboard.

From a historical perspective, [[W:controlled burning|controlled burning]] has long been practiced by the Indigenous peoples to allow the natural fires to run their course, which prevents devastating wildfires and allows for increased biodiversity. This [[W:Traditional ecological knowledge|traditional ecological knowledge]] has been preserved, although it is on a much smaller scale than what it was in the past: prior to 1850, about 9 million acres (34,000 km²) burned yearly, in fires that lasted for months, with wildfire activity peaking roughly every 30 years, when up to 11.8 million acres (47,753 km³) of land is burned. 

As of August 2023, more than 12 million people in Balisca live in areas sited completely within zones deemed to be at very high risk of fire (WUI). In total, more than 22.7 million people live in "at risk fire hazard zones", which also include areas that are less exposed.

==Culture and society==
{{Main|Society of Balisca|Baliscanismo}}

{{Further|Baliscan values|Baliscan popular culture|Public holidays in Balisca}}
