Whether or not Balisca should be considered a culturally {{W|Western world|Western country}} is hotly contested, and current consensus is that Balisca is "intimately related to the West", while simultaneously being recognized as an influential country in [[Abyala]] and the wider [[Algarves]]. Historically, Baliscan culture is marked by strong historic ties to [[Andalus]], the {{W|Levant}} and the {{W|Maghreb}}. This was followed by immigration from the wider {{W|Old World}} following independence which played pivotal roles in the country's formation and subsequent identity. Baliscan art, architecture, cuisine, and music have been shaped by successive waves of foreign immigrants, as well as by the country's diverse climate and geography.  

Today it is considered a [[W:Salad bowl (cultural idea)|salad bowl]] and [[W:Polyculturalism|polycultural society]], having developed a sophisticated culture and etiquette which absorbed Indigenous practices alongside influences brought from [[Kaftia]], [[Fosia]] and [[Illypnia]] beginning in the 16th century. During the colonial era, the prevailing culture was largely influenced by [[Ethnic groups in Illypnia|Illypnians]] (predominately [[W:Catalans|Volisanians]] and [[W:Occitans|Berons]]), along with other Mesogean colonists from the [[Empire of Burawa]] (now just [[Burawa]]), [[Aetolia]] and [[Orma]]. 

It's cities are largely characterized by both a diverse amount of ethnic and cultural diversity, and of conscious adaptation of international styles in fashion, architecture and design. Museums, cinemas, and galleries are abundant in all the large urban centers, as well as unorthodox establishments such as {{W|love hotel}}s, literary bars, [[W:Internet café|internet cafés]], or bars offering live music of a variety of genres with large elements of Indigenous and Kaftian influences, particularly in the fields of music (especially in hip-hop and rap) and art. 

[[File:Correfoc 2023.jpg|left|thumb|The tradition of [[W:Correfoc|''correfocs'']], "fire runs" was brought over to Balisca by Volisanian colonists. In the ''correfoc'', a group of individuals will dress as [[W:Devil|devils]] and light up [[W:Fireworks|fireworks]] – fixed on devil's [[W:pitchforks|pitchforks]] or strung above the route. Dancing to the sound of a rhythmic drum group, they set off their fireworks among crowds of spectators. The spectators that participate dress to protect themselves against small burns and attempt to get as close as possible to the devils, running with the fire. Other spectators will watch from "safe" distances, rapidly retreating as necessary.]]Similar to other Abyalan countries, Balisca is considered to be a [[w:High-context and low-context cultures|low-context culture]] by sociologists. Stemming from the significant influence of successive waves of immigrants in the post-independence development of the Baliscan nation, Balisca has adopted and absorbed foreign influences into its identity. With the arrival of {{W|Industrialization}} and the ''[[Cosmocazo]]'', Baliscan society was exposed to a wide array customs, ideas and trends arising around the world at the time. 

Overseas customs such as [[W:Dabke|dabke]], {{W|baseball}}, [[W:Belly dance|belly dance]], {{W|rugby}}, [[W:Comics|comics]] were adopted by an increasingly cosmopolitan society. These trends were in part a result of the [[W:Thassalocracy|thassalocratic]] nature of the colonial period. The pace of this "opening up" was solidified with the [[1883 Confederal Convention]] and hastened with the [[Baliscan Revolution]], in which several imposed cultural and religious norms and taboos were gradually eliminated. Post-revolution, these changes were embraced with gusto, and with the encouragement of the [[Government and politics of Balisca|confederal government]], rapidly took on a "Baliscan character" (termed ''baliscanización'').

While Balisca inherited significant influences from throughout the [[Mesogean world]] as a consequence of three centuries of colonization by [[Mesogean Basin|countries around the Mesogean]], contemporary Baliscan culture shares similarities with many Mesogean cultures. The most notable inheritances include the languages ([[Ajami]], [[W:Catalan language|Volisanian]], [[W:Occitan language|Beron]], Judaeo-Volisan ["Ladino"] and [[W:Aragonese language|Xacano]]), the predominant forms of {{W|Christianity}} ([[Catharism]] and {{W|Catholicism}}), [[W:Mediterranean cuisine|Mesogean cuisine]], and post-colonial architectural styles. There are strong by Kaftian and Indigenous traditions, as well as those of immigrants from other countries. Some aspects of Baliscan culture are contributions of nationalities such as [[Yanbarun Baliscans|Yanbarun]], [[Filipino Baliscans|Maharlikan]], [[Indian Baliscans|Kemali]], [[Sanese Baliscans|Sanese]] and other countries of origin. This diverse cultural background has helped boast many celebrations and festivals that have become known around the world. This has gradually given rise to a rich {{W|cultural mosaic}}, which has also made Balisca a popular destination for millions of international tourists each year. 

Balisca's geographic pre-disposition combined with its long tradition of tolerance and participatory approaches to {{W|participatory democracy|democracy}} and {{W|participatory economics|economics}} has made it strong cultural power in the area. Baliscan society typically rejects {{W|sectarianism}}, actively opposes {{W|moralism}} and {{W|religion in politics}}, {{W|hierarchy}}, while having a notable presence of risk taking and [[W:Free expression|free expression]]. Despite certain consistent ideological principles (e.g. {{W|Communitarianism}}, {{W|egalitarianism}}, and a uniquely stronger faith in {{W|commons}} and {{W|democracy}} than the rest of the region), Baliscan culture has a variety of expressions due to its geographical scale and demographic diversity. 

The Baliscan Confederation was originally divided culturally along ethnolinguistic lines (Volisanian, Xacano, Ajami, etc.) with immigrants contributing to but eventually assimilating into the predominating culture of the respective [[Communities of Balisca|republics]]. However, beginning in the 1880s and continuing on in the present day, the country has effectively shifted towards cultural diversity, pluralism, adopting the image of a [[w:salad bowl (cultural idea)|salad bowl]] or {{W|cultural mosaic}} instead. Throughout the country's history, certain cultures or subcultures (whether based on ethnicity or other commonality, such as the [[Indigenous nations in Balisca|Indigenous tribe]] or the {{W|gay village}}) have remained in certain communes or cantons, integrated in various degrees with broader culture. 

Since the end of the {{W|Cold War}} successive Baliscan governments have utilized {{W|trans-cultural diffusion}} in the realm of diplomacy to break down barriers and further {{W|internationalism|internationalist}} and {{W|humanitarianism|humanitarianist}} causes. Baliscan television dramas, called [[W:Telenovelas|''telenovelas'']] or ''jogonovelas'' ("play stories") have gained an extensive following across the Algarves, [[Illypnia]], [[Kaftia]] and [[Fosia]] (thanks to an increased interest in the Andalusian languages there). The spread and popularity of contemporary Baliscan social, fashion, and music trends as part of Balisca's internationalist policy has collectively become known as the ''Baliscola'' ({{Literal translation|Baliscan wave|Baliscan approach}}). [[Festival de la Flor y el Amor]] (Festival of Flower and Love) is symbolic of [[Rural society in Balisca|Rural Balisca.]] It is often compared to [[W:Valentine's Day|Valentine's Day]], and consists of two days (June 15-16) of festivities (colorful street decorations, parades and carnivals) in celebration of [[W:Fertility|fertility]], [[W:Sexuality|sexuality]] and [[W:Free love|free love]]. 

===Architecture and urban design===
{{Main|Architecture of Balisca}}

[[File:Jatiel Cabildo.png|left|thumb|300x300px|The Cabildo of [[Jatiel]] built in a Mudejar Revival influenced [[W:Constructivist architecture|Constructivist style]] ]]
[[File:Mission Sant-Maloù Park.png|thumb|250x250px|[[Mission Sant-Maloù Park]] ([[Trinacria]]), an example of [[W:Beaux-Arts architecture|Beaux-Arts architecture]] in [[Castejón]]]]
[[File:Ipemaraeté.png|thumb|right|[[Ipemaraeté]] ("place of the laughing flowers"; [[Hibiscara]])|250x250px]]
[[File:Confederal Style architecture, Sol.png|left|thumb|300x300px|[[Confederal Style architecture]] in [[Hisbanya]] ([[Boroughs of Castejón|Borough of Castejón]])]]
[[File:Basílica de Raymond Ajudant dels Càtars interior.png|left|thumb|300x300px|Interior of [[Basílica de Raymond Ajudant dels Càtars]] ([[Navassa]])]]
[[File:Pujol cityscape.png|thumb|250x250px|The design of the [[Pujol Clock Tower|clock tower]] of [[Pujol]] has significant {{W|Minaret|Islamic architectural}} influences.]]
Balisca has a very broad and diverse architectural style, which cannot be simply classified by period or region, due to several colonial division into various decentralized regions prior to [[Baliscan War of Independence|its independence]]. This has created a highly diverse and eclectic range in architectural designs. Styles range from a traditional and colonial aesthetic to contemporary modern architecture as it traverses the variety of cultures and landscapes that are spread throughout the country. The combination of Balisca’s natural geography, its urban predisposition and population of people of cosmopolitan heritage has ensured that it is a country whose architecture is wide-ranging. 

Colonial-era buildings are relatively rare in much of the country as a result of seismic activity, especially in the [[Alpujarra]] and the region around the [[Strait of Ivorra]]. [[Axarquía]] for example was almost completely destroyed by the [[1853 Gran Sur earthquake]].

Baliscan urban design has also heavily influenced Baliscan architecture, as early Baliscan settlements and their narrow street networks grew organically and emphasized {{W|walkability}} and connection to the community. Balisca has long lacked {{W|zoning}} laws, and this combined with the sustained influx of immigrants encouraged and electric variety of styles. Additionally {{W|urban vitality}} has long been emphasized in cities. As such, despite being admired for being picturesque, Baliscan urban areas can be confusing for visitors. Similarly confusing to many are the plans of more modern districts with discontinuous and curvilinear streets. Change of street orientation, particularly when gradual or arbitrary, is common in Baliscan cities, as are impasses, crescents or cul-de-sacs. 

In the 19th century a new focus emerged: engineering efforts to achieve a new language and bring about structural improvements using iron and glass as the main building materials, and the academic focus, firstly on revivals and {{W|Eclecticism in architecture|eclecticism}}, and later on regionalism. With the appointment of [[Michelangelo Valentino]] and the new progressive ''[[Anyos Federales]]'', the homegrown, pan-Baliscan ''[[Cosmocazo]]'' came into popularity. It is characterized by its eclecticism, because of the mix of decorative elements from various [[Old World]] styles, namely {{W|Neo-Mudéjar}}, {{W|Moorish Revival architecture|Moorish Revival}} and {{W|Palazzo style}}, and widespread use of {{W|Architectural terracotta|architectural ceramics}} and oriental motifs. The arrival of the arrival of new ''{{W|Modernisme}}'' to the Baliscan academic arena was relatively quick thanks to the historic cultural ties to the [[Mesogean world]], and heavily influenced Baliscan architectural development.

While there are also numerous examples of {{W|Neoclassical architecture|Neoclassical}}, {{W|Renaissance Revival}} and {{W|Georgian architecture|Georgian}} and {{W|Art Deco}}, these styles quickly fell out of popularity or were absorbed into that of newer concepts. In the more isolated regions of northern Balisca, one can find a traditional {{W|ruca}} (or ruka), a Wallche home made of wood and covered in hay. Baliscan architecture’s penchant for contemporary dwellings, built using concrete, glass and steel, has placed the country firmly on the modern-day architectural map. Nature imposes a variety of conditions on Baliscan architecture: across the country, there are few flat areas (the exception being the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|Western Plains]]) mostly near the coasts, with steep areas close to the mountains, and wet, forested areas in close proximity to each other. Moreover, a significant portion of the country is seismically active, which introduces additional challenges to construction.

After the [[Baliscan Revolution|social revolution]] in 1918-19, Baliscan architects continued to reimagine traditional styles and techniques. Balisca had an enormous shortage of housing after the war due to an influx of new immigrants and the return of soldiers from overseas which sparked a subsequent {{W|baby boom}}. Within the two years after the end of [[Great Adonian War]], 1.6 million housing units were constructed. Nevertheless, with the arrival of the 1970s, Baliscan architecture rapidly leaned away from old Illypnian-influenced styles and created new aesthetic designs as the country confronted the [[Special Period]]. The accelerated development of [[Trinacria]] would see a dedicated great experiment in {{W|modernist architecture}}. 

In regards to {{W|contemporary architecture|contemporary}} Baliscan architecture, it is largely shaped by environmental concerns and increasing urbanization, which has resulted in {{W|verticalization}}. Subcultures such as ''[[Desconexión]]'' have largely broken into the architectural scene, influencing not only urban, but suburban and rural settings. Even with the introduction of stronger {{W|urban planning}} measures in the 1970s, walkability and {{W|cyclibility}} have remained heavily prioritized over {{W|motor vehicles}} through new concepts such as {{W|complete communities}} and the {{W|15-minute city}}. At present, there has been a strong current of [[Cosmocazo#Architecture|Cosmopolitano]] design with more visible {{W|Palazzo style architecture|Palazzo}} and contemporary influences, known as [[Neo-Cosmopolitano|Nuevo Cosmopolitano]] Style. 

