=== Environmentalism ===
[[File:Baliscan conservationist rangers.png|thumb|250x250px|Baliscan conservationist rangers pose for a photo in the [[Araribá Valley]] in the [[Hibiscara]] region in 1924.]]
The territory of Balisca boasts a rich and longstanding culture of [[W:Conservationism|conservationism]] and [[W:Environmentalism|environmentalism]], along with more recent [[Ecological Action|radical action]]. This island nation has garnered global recognition for its unwavering commitment to preserving its natural resources and nurturing a harmonious relationship between humans and the environment. The conservation efforts in Balisca are deeply intertwined with the protection of [[W:Indigenous sovereignty|Indigenous sovereignty]], and it is through the stewardship of Indigenous communities that the vast majority of the country's nature has been safeguarded.

At the core of Balisca's conservation and environmentalism is the cultural and legal respect for [[Indigenous nations in Balisca|Indigenous sovereignty]]. Indigenous communities have inhabited Balisca for centuries prior to Illypnian colonization, fostering a deep connection with the land and an innate understanding of its ecological intricacies. Recognizing the wisdom [[W:Traditional ecological knowledge|embedded in Indigenous knowledge systems]], the nation's policies have actively involved Indigenous groups in conservation decision-making processes. Balisca's Indigenous communities are credited with being the first guardians of Balisca's unique biodiversity. By leveraging traditional ecological practices and sustainable resource management, they have succeeded in protecting vast swaths of pristine ecosystems. Their deeply rooted respect and reverence for the environment has translated into a commitment to maintaining ecological balance, conserving biodiversity, and ensuring the longevity of their ancestral lands.
[[File:Xàbia flooded paddy field.png|left|thumb|Restored [[W:Rice paddy|rice paddies]] on the edge of {{W|Urbanization|urban}} development in [[Xàbia]]. [[Rural society in Balisca|Rural society]] in Balisca has embraced {{W|densification}} in an attempt to minimize {{W|Human impact on the environment|ecological impact}}.]]
Balisca's conservation journey can be traced back to the early 20th century when Balisca experienced rapid industrialization and urbanization. Fearing the potential loss of their unique natural heritage, activists and local leaders spearheaded the formation of grassroots conservation groups. These early efforts paved the way for the establishment of Balisca's first [[W:nature reserve|nature reserves]] and [[W:Protected area|protected areas]], preserving key habitats and critical biodiversity hotspots. As the decades unfolded, the collaboration between cantons, regions, and at the confederal level with direction from indigenous communities have gained significant momentum. The confederation gradually implemented progressive environmental policies that not only prioritized habitat preservation but also emphasized [[W:Sustainability|sustainable resource use]], [[W:Pollution control|pollution control]], and {{W|indigenous decolonization}}.

In the contemporary era, Balisca's conservation ethos has evolved to encompass a holistic approach that has tried to address both natural and human concerns, while not infringing on their latter. Balisca has become a global leader in advocating for the concept of "bio-cultural conservation," which recognizes the interdependence between [[W:Cultural diversity|cultural diversity]] and [[W:Ecological health|ecological vitality]]. This perspective underscores the importance of preserving [[W:Traditional ecological knowledge|Indigenous traditions, and knowledge alongside the protection of natural landscapes]]. 

Balisca's commitment to conservation and environmentalism is evident through its investments in [[W:Renewable energy|renewable energy]] infrastructure, [[W:Eco-tourism|eco-tourism]] initiatives, and [[W:Environmental education|educational programs]]. These efforts have not only helped to raise awareness of human impact on the environment, but also empowered local communities to actively participate in safeguarding their environment.

=== ''Desconexión'' ===
{{Main|Desconexión}}

[[File:Avante poster.jpg|thumb|342x342px|A 2024 poster from the ''[[Avante!]]'' anarchist collective, an example of Baliscan [[W:Détournement|''détournement'']] ]]
''Desconexión'' is an [[Baliscano language|Baliscano]] word that roughly translates as "disconnection" or "unplugging". This term is used to describe a widespread Baliscan [[w:counterculture|counter]]/{{W|subculture}} known for its strongly {{W|anti-consumerism|anti-consumerist}} and {{W|social anarchism|social anarchist}} and {{W|hedonism|hedonistic}} ideals. It has become associated with the {{W|NEET}} demographic, of which youth make up a disproportionate percentage. While often labelled "{{W|hippie}}", it originated in the [[Special Period]]-era hardships faced by the overwhelmingly young [[W:NEET|NEET]] Baliscans struggling with social and economic struggles (particularly [[Baliscan housing shortage|housing insecurity]]) in [[Araucaria]] and other areas of the confederation.

Desconexión is considered a uniquely Baliscan phenomenon, pairing the {{W|grunge}}, {{W|beatnik}}, {{W|hippie}} and {{W|Sumak kawsay|buen vivir}} concepts with rebel movements from past eras with the {{W|Anarcho-naturism|their rejection}} of today’s {{W|industrialisation|industrialized}} {{W|mass society}}. It draws significant inspiration from the diverse cultural and ethnic background of the Baliscan Confederation, reflecting the misfortune of communities left behind by the country's rapid socioeconomic transformation. In contemporary Baliscan society it has increasingly dominated over more Illypnian and [[W:Bohemian style|Bohemian]] influences that are typically dominant in the rest of the world. It's rapid rise into mainstream Baliscan society has led to a blending of beauty and fashion trends from across the world, leading to occasional accusations of [[W:Cultural appropriation|cultural appropriation]]. Desconexión is closely associated with {{W|Permissive society|permissive}} beliefs, such as [[W:Sexual revolution|sexual liberation]], {{W|free love}}, {{W|post-genderism}}, {{W|anarchist egoism|egoism}}, {{W|anti-statism}}, {{W|recreational drug use}}, and {{W|Dealignment|political dealignment}}. Followers of the trend (called ''desconexiónistas''; "unplugged") typically have a strong distaste for [[W:Electoralism|electoralism]], and are more aligned with [[W:Direct action|direct action]] and grassroots organization.

On average, ''desconexiónistas'' are happier than typical Baliscan society, experience lower levels of stress, and have lower disposable and discretionary incomes. The subculture is notable for its higher level of drug consumption than the average Baliscan; 92% of its followers reported smoking {{W|cannabis (drug)|cannabis}} at least once a week, while 61% responded that they consumed alcohol at least once a week. 42% reported smoking nicotine cigarettes at least once a month, and 24% reported using {{W|psilocybin mushrooms}} at least once in the month prior. Desconexión accelerated the Baliscan {{W|Critique of work|anti-work movement}} and has embraced tenets of [[W:Illegalism|illegalism]], which has also fueled illicit activity including [[Baliscan Mafia|Mafia-related]] crime and [[Cybercrime in Balisca|cybercrime]]. It played a significant role in the [[2021 Baliscan civil unrest]], and influenced the push for the recognition of [[Autonomous commune (Balisca)|autonomous communes]]. Baliscans that subscribe to desconexión actively choose to lower their professional and economic ambitions and simplify their goals, still being responsible for their own essential needs, and prioritize psychological health over [[W:Economic materialism|economic materialism]]. There is a strong criticism of [[W:Wage slavery|wage slavery]] and such, the desconexión movement has been blamed as the main driver of Balisca's high youth unemployment rate. 

===Entertainment and popular culture===
[[File:Ateo MV.jpg|thumb|265x265px|{{W|Nathy Peluso}} and [[W:C.Tangana|C.Tangana]] representing Balisca at the [[AINVision Arriola 2020|2022 AINVision Song Contest]] with "[[W:Ateo|Ateo]]"]]
[[File:Scarlata Rosari.png|left|thumb|284x284px|The cover of [[Scarlata Rosari]], one of the first Baliscan {{W|manga|magina}} that gained broad popularity in the 1980s]]
Balisca popular culture not only reflects the [[Desconexión|attitudes and concerns]] of the present day but also provides a link to the past. Popular films, television programs, books, music, animation (called ''animato'') and video games all developed from older artistic and literary traditions, and many of their themes and styles of presentation can be traced to traditional art forms. Contemporary forms of popular culture, provide not only entertainment but also an escape for the contemporary Baliscan from the problems of an industrial world. ''Magina'' (plural ''magini''; literally 'drawn strips') are comics that originated in Balisca in the 1980s and take some inspiration from {{W|manga}}, which originated in [[Sanese Baliscans|Sanese immigrant communities]] in urban centers. 

During the Baliscan economic malaise, The growing international popularity of anime has led to various animation studios from other countries making their own anime-influenced works. The Baliscan animation market has also been described as owing greatly to the crucial role of fans as cultural agents, the [[W:Deterritorialization|deterritorializing]] effects of {{W|globalization}}, the domestication and heavy promotion of animation of anime to suit local tastes, and being part of the wider global exchange {{W|pop culture}} and "soft power". It is argued that the rise of animation in Balisca is a result of the sophisticated graphic quality, a wide thematic diversity, and the Baliscan mentality which rejects the Disney convention of a {{W|happy ending}} and aimed to provide a more realistic image of the world.

By the late 1980s, the community was the focus of leisure activities, such as excursions to parks or shopping districts. Balisca is known to be a country that values [[W:Community building|community building]] and [[W:Interculturalism|interculturalism]], with a population that heavily values [[W:Kindness|kindness]] and [[W:Collective|collectivity]]. Sometimes described as having a "{{W|All work and no play makes Jack a dull boy|all play and no work}}" mentality, the Baliscan population has been known to seek enjoyment wherever they can and maximize {{W|leisure}}. It is common to see Baliscan commuters riding the train to work, enjoying their favorite books or shows, or listening through earphones to the latest in {{W|popular music}}, or the wide variety of types of popular entertainment that is available. There is a large selection of music, films, and the products of a huge animation industry, among other forms of entertainment, from which to choose. Game centers, [[W:Bowling alley|bowling alleys]], and [[W:Karaoke|karaoke]] are popular hangout places for teens while older people may go to bars, clubs, {{W|love hotel}}s or street events which are increasingly common. Together, the publishing, film/video, music/audio, and game industries in Balisca make up the growing Baliscan content industry.

=== Sport ===
{{Further|Balisca national football team}}
[[w:Association football|Association football]] has long been the most popular sport in Balisca, existing in several different forms since ancient times. As of 2022, there are 4,631,238 registered players playing in football leagues. The [[Baliscan Football Association]] ({{lang-bl|Federación Baliscana de Futbol}}) has over 64,000 teams. Baliscan football clubs such as [[Atlètic Navaza]], [[E.L. Póvoa]], [[A.C. Alessandria]] and [[Reial Mercè]] (known as the "Big Four") are the most popular teams and enjoy the reputation as some of the most successful clubs. These clubs all participate in the [[La Liga (Balisca)|''La Liga'']] system. The Baliscan Football Association was founded in 1922 and since then has dedicated its time and effort to promoting the game, organizing youth programs and improving the abilities of not only its players, but of the officials and coaches involved with its regional teams.
[[File:Samir Aragão .png|thumb|270x270px|[[Samir Aragão]] is all-time leading goalscorer amongst all Baliscan clubs, with 290 goals in 379 appearances]]
[[File:Capoeira.jpg|left|thumb|300x300px|''Capoeiristas'' practicing [[W:Capoeira|capoeira]] at a beach in [[Macaio]]]]
Football rivalries are prevalent in Balisca, and often take a {{W|regionalism|regionalist}} or {{W|nationalist}} approach, representative of the [[regions of Balisca|region]] or [[Communities of Balisca|community]] from which they originate. ''[[El Clásico]]'' ("The Classic") is the name given in football to any match between fierce rivals [[Atlètic Navaza]] and [[E.L. Póvoa]]. Other than the {{W|Copa Libertadores}}, it is considered one of the biggest club football games in the Balisca, and is among the most viewed annual sporting events. A fixture known for its intensity, it has featured {{W|Goal celebration|memorable goal celebrations}} from both teams, often involving mocking the opposition, along with {{W|Football hooliganism|football hooliganism}} perpetrated by the two clubs' fans. The ''El Clásico'' rivalry comes about from historical [[Reapers' War|animosity between Volisanians and Xacanos]] in Balisca, and they are sometimes identified with opposing political positions, with Atlètic Navaza viewed as representing [[Volisanian nationalism]] and Póvoa viewed as representing [[Xacano people#Balisca|Chacano nationalism]]. 

The most popular sporting event native to Balisca is the confederal [[Baliscan Spartakiad|Spartakiad]] ({{lang-bl|Espartaquiada}}), which are held every two years (alternating between Winter and Summer editions), which features a team from each of Balisca's forty-five regions competing against each other to win the most medals. The event was last hosted by [[Macaio]] ([[Carpinchera]]) in 2024. Other confederation-wide sports events include the ''[[Copa Confederación]]'' (Confederation Cup), which is an annual football competition held between the [[List of football clubs in Balisca|Balisca football clubs]], to determine which team heads to the {{W|Copa Libertadores}}. Balisca is also a participant in the [[Pan-Abyalan Games]], a {{W|multi-sport event}} consisting of the 47 members of the [[Panabyala Sports|Panabyala Sports Organization]].

{{W|Rugby union}}, tennis, cycling, handball, {{W|futsal}}, {{W|basketball}} and, lately, {{W|Formula One}} are also important due to the presence of Baliscan champions in all these disciplines. Today, Balisca is a major world sports powerhouse, especially since the [[1982 Pan-Abyalan Games|2012 Pan-Abyalan Games]] that were hosted in [[Castejón]], which stimulated a great deal of interest in sports in the country. The tourism industry has led to an improvement in sports infrastructure, especially for water sports, golf and skiing. Traditional sports such as [[w:Basque pelota|Harrian pelota]] has also seen a massive revival.

